To create a deep copy of an object in JavaScript, you can use the `JSON.stringify()` and `JSON.parse()` methods. However, these methods are not capable of handling objects with circular references, as they will throw an error.

To handle circular references, you can use a `Map` object to keep track of visited objects and their copies. Here's an example implementation of a function that creates a deep copy of an object, even if it contains circular references:

```javascript
function deepCopy(obj, visited = new Map()) {
  // Check if obj is a primitive value or not an object
  if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') {
    return obj;
  }

  // Check if obj has already been visited and return the copy
  if (visited.has(obj)) {
    return visited.get(obj);
  }

  // Create an empty copy of obj
  const copy = Array.isArray(obj) ? [] : {};

  // Add obj to the visited map
  visited.set(obj, copy);

  // Iterate over all properties of obj
  Object.keys(obj).forEach(key => {
    // Recursively deep copy each property
    copy[key] = deepCopy(obj[key], visited);
  });

  return copy;
}
```

