Here's a C++ function that prints the Fibonacci series using recursion and memoization:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>

// Function to calculate the nth Fibonacci number using recursion and memoization
unsigned long long fibonacci(int n, std::unordered_map<int, unsigned long long>& memo) {
    // Base cases
    if (n == 0)
        return 0;
    if (n == 1 || n == 2)
        return 1;

    // Check if the result is already computed and stored in the memo
    if (memo.find(n) != memo.end())
        return memo[n];

    // Calculate the Fibonacci number recursively
    unsigned long long fib = fibonacci(n - 1, memo) + fibonacci(n - 2, memo);

    // Store the result in the memo for future use
    memo[n] = fib;

    return fib;
}

// Function to print the Fibonacci series up to the given number
void printFibonacciSeries(int n) {
    std::unordered_map<int, unsigned long long> memo;

    std::cout << "Fibonacci Series up to " << n << ":" << std::endl;
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        unsigned long long fib = fibonacci(i, memo);
        std::cout << fib << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    int n = 10; // Number of Fibonacci numbers to print

    printFibonacciSeries(n);

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the `fibonacci` function is implemented to calculate the nth Fibonacci number using recursion and memoization. It takes an integer `n` and a reference to an `unordered_map` called `memo` as parameters. The `memo` is used to store the computed Fibonacci numbers to avoid redundant calculations.

The `printFibonacciSeries` function is used to print the Fibonacci series up to the given number. It initializes an empty `unordered_map` called `memo` and then iterates from 0 to `n`, calling the `fibonacci` function to calculate each Fibonacci number and print it.

In the `main` function, the `printFibonacciSeries` function is called with a value of `n` set to 10, which will print the first 10 Fibonacci numbers.

