```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class BubbleSort {

    public static <T extends Comparable<T>> T[] bubbleSort(T[] array) {
        T[] sortedArray = Arrays.copyOf(array, array.length);

        for (int i = 0; i < sortedArray.length - 1; i++) {
            boolean swapped = false;
            for (int j = 0; j < sortedArray.length - i - 1; j++) {
                if (sortedArray[j].compareTo(sortedArray[j + 1]) > 0) {
                    T temp = sortedArray[j];
                    sortedArray[j] = sortedArray[j + 1];
                    sortedArray[j + 1] = temp;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
            if (!swapped) {
                break;
            }
        }

        return sortedArray;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer[] array = {5, -2, 10, 0, 3, 1};
        Integer[] sortedArray = bubbleSort(array);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(sortedArray));
    }
}
```

This implementation uses generics to make the bubble sort algorithm applicable to arrays of any data type that implements the Comparable interface. It creates a new array called sortedArray to hold the sorted elements, so the original array is not modified.

To optimize the algorithm, a boolean variable swapped is used to track if any elements were swapped during each pass. If no elements were swapped, it means the array is already sorted and the algorithm can exit early.

The implementation includes a main method with a sample usage, sorting an array of integers.

To parallelize the sorting algorithm, you can make use of Java's ExecutorService and multi-threading capabilities. Here's an example of how you can modify the bubbleSort method to use multiple threads:

```java
public static <T extends Comparable<T>> T[] parallelBubbleSort(T[] array) {
    T[] sortedArray = Arrays.copyOf(array, array.length);
    ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());

    for (int i = 0; i < sortedArray.length - 1; i++) {
        boolean swapped = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < sortedArray.length - i - 1; j++) {
            int finalJ = j;
            executor.submit(() -> {
                if (sortedArray[finalJ].compareTo(sortedArray[finalJ + 1]) > 0) {
                    T temp = sortedArray[finalJ];
                    sortedArray[finalJ] = sortedArray[finalJ + 1];
                    sortedArray[finalJ + 1] = temp;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            });
        }

        executor.shutdown();
        try {
            executor.awaitTermination(Long.MAX_VALUE, TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        if (!swapped) {
            break;
        }
    }

    return sortedArray;
}
```

In this modified version, the inner loop of the bubble sort algorithm is parallelized by submitting each comparison and swap as a separate task to the executor. The executor is then shutdown and awaited for all tasks to complete before checking if any swaps were made and exiting early if not.

