Here's an example code to create a list of 10,000 strings in Java, ensuring that none of the strings have any repeating characters:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Random;

public class UniqueStringList {
    private static final int LIST_SIZE = 10000;
    private static final int STRING_LENGTH = 10;
    private static final String CHARACTERS = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789";

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> uniqueStringList = generateUniqueStrings();
        // Printing first 10 strings for demonstration
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(uniqueStringList.get(i));
        }
    }

    private static List<String> generateUniqueStrings() {
        List<String> uniqueStrings = new ArrayList<>();
        Random random = new Random();

        while (uniqueStrings.size() < LIST_SIZE) {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (int i = 0; i < STRING_LENGTH; i++) {
                int randomIndex = random.nextInt(CHARACTERS.length());
                char randomChar = CHARACTERS.charAt(randomIndex);
                sb.append(randomChar);
            }
            String randomString = sb.toString();
            if (!hasDuplicateCharacters(randomString) && !uniqueStrings.contains(randomString)) {
                uniqueStrings.add(randomString);
            }
        }
        return uniqueStrings;
    }

    private static boolean hasDuplicateCharacters(String string) {
        for (int i = 0; i < string.length(); i++) {
            if (string.lastIndexOf(string.charAt(i)) != i) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

This code generates a list of 10,000 unique strings, where each string has a length of 10 characters. The `CHARACTERS` string includes all alphanumeric characters in both uppercase and lowercase.

The `generateUniqueStrings()` method uses a `while` loop to keep generating random strings until the desired list size is achieved. It uses a `StringBuilder` to construct each random string by randomly selecting characters from the `CHARACTERS` string. The `hasDuplicateCharacters()` method is used to check if a generated string has any repeating characters.

Note that generating 10,000 unique strings with no repeating characters can take some time, so the code may take a while to execute.

