The `printPyramidPattern` function uses a while loop to iterate through each row. In each iteration, it calculates the sum of all numbers in the row and checks if it is a perfect square. If it is, it constructs the row string with spaces and prints it.

The `getNextPrime` function finds the next prime number by starting from the last prime number found and incrementing by 2 until a prime number is found. The `isPrime` function checks if a number is prime by iterating from 2 to the square root of the number and checking if the number is divisible by any of the iteration numbers.

You can change the parameter in the `printPyramidPattern` function call at the end to print the pyramid pattern for a different number of rows.

