One way to sort elements of an array in descending order without using any built-in sorting methods in Java is to implement a simple sorting algorithm like Bubble Sort or Selection Sort.

Here is an example using Bubble Sort:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {5, 2, 10, 8, 3};

        // Sorting in descending order using Bubble Sort
        bubbleSortDescending(array);

        // Printing the sorted array
        for (int num : array) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }

    public static void bubbleSortDescending(int[] array) {
        int n = array.length;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (array[j] < array[j + 1]) {
                    // Swapping the elements
                    int temp = array[j];
                    array[j] = array[j + 1];
                    array[j + 1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Output:
```
10 8 5 3 2
```

In the above example, we have implemented the Bubble Sort algorithm to sort the array in descending order. The inner loop compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. The outer loop repeats this process until the array is fully sorted.

Here's a Java code that generates 20 random prime numbers between -100 and 100, computes their sum and average, displays the highest and lowest prime numbers, and sorts the prime numbers in ascending order:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Random;

public class RandomPrimeNumbers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> primeNumbers = new ArrayList<>();
        Random random = new Random();

        while (primeNumbers.size() < 20) {
            int number = random.nextInt(201) - 100; // Generate a random number between -100 and 100
            if (isPrime(number)) {
                primeNumbers.add(number);
            }
        }

        int sum = 0;
        int lowest = primeNumbers.get(0);
        int highest = primeNumbers.get(0);

        for (int primeNumber : primeNumbers) {
            sum += primeNumber;
            lowest = Math.min(lowest, primeNumber);
            highest = Math.max(highest, primeNumber);
        }

        double average = (double) sum / primeNumbers.size();

        System.out.println("Generated Prime Numbers: " + primeNumbers);
        System.out.println("Sum of Prime Numbers: " + sum);
        System.out.println("Average of Prime Numbers: " + average);
        System.out.println("Lowest Prime Number: " + lowest);
        System.out.println("Highest Prime Number: " + highest);

        Collections.sort(primeNumbers);
        System.out.println("Sorted Prime Numbers: " + primeNumbers);
    }

    // Method to check if a number is prime
    public static boolean isPrime(int number) {
        if (number <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
            if (number % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
