Here is an example implementation of a program for HTML input validation in Python:

```python
import re

class HTMLValidator:
    def __init__(self):
        self.stack = []

    def validate(self, html):
        errors = []
        self.stack = []

        tags = re.findall(r'<\s*([^/\s>]+)', html)
        for tag in tags:
            if not tag.startswith('/'):  # opening tag
                self.stack.append(tag)
            else:  # closing tag
                if not self.stack:
                    errors.append(f"Found closing tag '{tag}' without corresponding opening tag")
                elif tag[1:] != self.stack[-1]:
                    errors.append(f"Found closing tag '{tag}' without matching opening tag")
                else:
                    self.stack.pop()

        if self.stack:
            errors.extend([f"Found unclosed tag '{tag}'" for tag in self.stack])

        attributes = re.findall(r'<\s*\w+\s*([^>]*)>', html)
        for attribute_str in attributes:
            attribute_pairs = re.findall(r'(\w+)\s*=\s*"([^"]*)"', attribute_str)
            for name, value in attribute_pairs:
                if not self.is_alphanumeric(name):
                    errors.append(f"Invalid attribute name '{name}'")
                if not self.is_quoted(value):
                    errors.append(f"Attribute value '{value}' should be enclosed in quotes")

        # TODO: Implement XSS attack detection and other security vulnerability checks

        return errors

    def is_alphanumeric(self, string):
        return bool(re.match(r'^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$', string))

    def is_quoted(self, string):
        return bool(re.match(r'^".*"$', string))

# Example usage
validator = HTMLValidator()
html = '<div><p>Some text</p><img src="image.jpg" alt="image"></div>'
errors = validator.validate(html)
if errors:
    for error in errors:
        print(error)
else:
    print("HTML is valid")
```

This example implementation uses regular expressions to extract tags and attributes from the HTML input. It maintains a stack to keep track of the opening tags and checks if every opening tag has a corresponding closing tag. It also validates the syntax and values of attributes. To handle nested tags, it ensures that each nested tag is closed before its parent tag. It currently does not implement XSS attack detection and other security vulnerability checks, but you can add those as additional functionality.

To optimize performance, you can consider using a more efficient HTML parsing library like BeautifulSoup instead of regular expressions. This will help handle large HTML inputs more effectively.

To ensure accuracy and reliability, you can write unit tests to cover different scenarios, such as valid HTML, missing closing tags, incorrect attribute syntax, etc.

