```javascript
function getLastKItems(array, k) {
  if (!Array.isArray(array) || array.length === 0 || k <= 0) {
    return [];
  }

  let lastKItems = [];

  for (let i = array.length - 1; i >= 0 && lastKItems.length < k; i--) {
    const currentItem = array[i];

    if (Array.isArray(currentItem)) {
      const nestedItems = getLastKItems(currentItem, k - lastKItems.length);
      lastKItems = nestedItems.concat(lastKItems);
    } else {
      lastKItems.unshift(currentItem);
    }
  }

  return lastKItems;
}
```

This function takes two parameters: `array` (the array from which to extract the last K items) and `k` (the number of items to extract).

The function first checks if `array` is a valid array, whether it is empty, and if `k` is a positive integer. If any of these conditions are not met, it returns an empty array.

Next, it initializes an empty array `lastKItems` to store the last K items.

Then, it iterates backwards through the `array` using a for loop. For each item, it checks if it is an array itself. If it is, it recursively calls the `getLastKItems` function with the nested array and the remaining number of items to extract (`k - lastKItems.length`), and appends the returned items to `lastKItems` using the `concat` method.

If the current item is not an array, it is directly prepended to `lastKItems` using the `unshift` method.

The loop continues until either `k` items are extracted or there are no more items in the `array`.

Finally, the function returns `lastKItems`, which contains the last K items of the array as a flattened array.

