```java
public static int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {
    int length = nums.length;
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
        boolean isDuplicate = false;

        for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[j]) {
                isDuplicate = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (!isDuplicate) {
            nums[count] = nums[i];
            count++;
        }
    }

    return count;
}
```

Explanation:
1. We start by initializing `length` as the length of the input array `nums` and `count` as 0. `count` will keep track of the number of unique elements in the array.
2. We use two nested loops to compare each element `nums[i]` with all the previous elements `nums[j]`.
3. If `nums[i]` is equal to any of the previous elements `nums[j]`, we set the boolean variable `isDuplicate` to true and break out of the inner loop.
4. If `isDuplicate` is false, it means `nums[i]` is a unique element. We update the element at index `count` in `nums` with `nums[i]` and increment `count` by 1.
5. After iterating through all the elements, we return `count`, which represents the number of unique elements in the array.

