To find the median of two sorted arrays efficiently, we can use the concept of binary search.

First, we need to ensure that arr1 is always the smaller array. If not, we can swap arr1 and arr2.

Next, we initialize two variables, low and high, to represent the boundaries of the binary search in arr1. The initial low is 0 and the initial high is the length of arr1.

Now, we start the binary search loop. In each iteration, we calculate the partition indices, mid1 and mid2, in arr1 and arr2 respectively. The partition indices divide the arrays into two halves.

To ensure that the median is always in the left half of the combined array, we want the number of elements in the left half to be equal to (n + m + 1) / 2, where n is the length of arr1 and m is the length of arr2. This is because if n + m is odd, the median is the middle element, and if n + m is even, the median is the average of the two middle elements.

If the maximum element in the left half of arr1 is greater than the minimum element in the right half of arr2, it means the partition is too far to the right in arr1. We need to move the partition to the left in arr1 by decreasing the high. Otherwise, if the maximum element in the left half of arr2 is greater than the minimum element in the right half of arr1, it means the partition is too far to the left in arr1. We need to move the partition to the right in arr1 by increasing the low.

If the partitions are at the correct positions, we can calculate the median. If n + m is odd, the median is the maximum of the maximum element in the left half of arr1 and the maximum element in the left half of arr2. If n + m is even, the median is the average of the maximum of the maximum element in the left half of arr1 and the maximum element in the left half of arr2, and the minimum of the minimum element in the right half of arr1 and the minimum element in the right half of arr2.

Here is the implementation in JavaScript:

```javascript
function medianOfTwoSortedArrays(arr1, arr2) {
  // Ensure arr1 is the smaller array
  if (arr1.length > arr2.length) {
    [arr1, arr2] = [arr2, arr1];
  }

  const n = arr1.length;
  const m = arr2.length;
  const totalLength = n + m;

  let low = 0;
  let high = n;

  while (low <= high) {
    const mid1 = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);
    const mid2 = Math.floor((totalLength + 1) / 2) - mid1;

    const maxLeft1 = (mid1 === 0) ? -Infinity : arr1[mid1 - 1];
    const maxLeft2 = (mid2 === 0) ? -Infinity : arr2[mid2 - 1];

    const minRight1 = (mid1 === n) ? Infinity : arr1[mid1];
    const minRight2 = (mid2 === m) ? Infinity : arr2[mid2];

    if (maxLeft1 <= minRight2 && maxLeft2 <= minRight1) {
      if (totalLength % 2 === 0) {
        return (Math.max(maxLeft1, maxLeft2) + Math.min(minRight1, minRight2)) / 2;
      } else {
        return Math.max(maxLeft1, maxLeft2);
      }
    } else if (maxLeft1 > minRight2) {
      high = mid1 - 1;
    } else {
      low = mid1 + 1;
    }
  }

  throw new Error("Arrays are not sorted!");
}
```

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log(min(n, m))) since we are performing a binary search on the smaller array.

