Determining the exact data type of a lambda expression can be challenging due to type inference and erasure. However, we can use reflection and some heuristics to make an educated guess about the data type. Keep in mind that this approach may not always yield accurate results, especially in complex scenarios. Here's an example implementation in Java:

```java
import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class LambdaDataTypeInference {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Function<Integer, String> lambda = x -> String.valueOf(x * 2);
        Class<?> dataType = inferLambdaDataType(lambda);
        System.out.println("Data type: " + dataType.getName());
    }

    public static Class<?> inferLambdaDataType(Object lambda) {
        Class<?> lambdaClass = lambda.getClass();

        if (lambdaClass.isSynthetic()) {
            Method[] methods = lambdaClass.getMethods();

            List<Method> functionalInterfaceMethods = Arrays.stream(methods)
                    .filter(method -> method.getDeclaringClass().equals(Object.class))
                    .collect(Collectors.toList());

            if (functionalInterfaceMethods.size() == 1) {
                Method method = functionalInterfaceMethods.get(0);
                return method.getReturnType();
            }
        }

        return lambdaClass.getInterfaces()[0].getDeclaredMethods()[0].getReturnType();
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a lambda expression `x -> String.valueOf(x * 2)` of type `Function<Integer, String>`. The `inferLambdaDataType` method attempts to infer the data type of the lambda expression.

First, we check if the lambda class is synthetic. Synthetic classes are generated by the compiler when handling nested lambda expressions. If the class is synthetic, we retrieve the functional interface method using reflection. If there is only one functional interface method, we assume that it is the intended method and return its return type.

If the lambda class is not synthetic, we assume that the lambda expression is a standalone lambda. In this case, we retrieve the first interface implemented by the lambda class and obtain its declared methods. We assume that the first method is the functional interface method and return its return type.

Note that this approach has limitations, especially when dealing with nested lambda expressions or more complex scenarios. The type inference may not always yield accurate results due to type erasure or other factors.

