Here is a Java program that generates a pyramid pattern of numbers based on the user's input:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class PyramidPattern {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter the number of rows: ");
        int numRows = scanner.nextInt();

        int[][] pyramid = new int[numRows][numRows];

        pyramid[0][0] = 1;  // Set the top of the pyramid to 1

        // Generate the pyramid pattern
        for (int i = 1; i < numRows; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
                if (j == 0 || j == i) {
                    pyramid[i][j] = 1;  // The first and last element in each row is 1
                } else {
                    pyramid[i][j] = pyramid[i-1][j-1] + pyramid[i-1][j];  // Sum of the two numbers above
                }
            }
        }

        // Display the pyramid pattern
        for (int i = 0; i < numRows; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
                System.out.print(pyramid[i][j] + " ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, we first prompt the user to enter the number of rows for the pyramid. Then, we create a 2D array called `pyramid` to store the numbers in the pyramid pattern.

We initialize the top of the pyramid to 1 and then use nested loops to generate the rest of the pyramid. The inner loop iterates from 0 to `i` (inclusive) to generate each element in the current row. For the first and last element in each row, we set it to 1. For the other elements, we calculate their value as the sum of the two numbers directly above them (`pyramid[i-1][j-1]` and `pyramid[i-1][j]`).

Finally, we display the pyramid pattern by iterating over the 2D array and printing each element.

