In Java, the `==` operator compares the memory addresses of two objects to determine if they are the same object, whereas the `equals()` method compares the actual content of two objects to check if they are equal. Here is an example code snippet that demonstrates the usage of `==` and `equals()` methods for comparing strings:

```java
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = new String("Hello");

// Using ==
boolean areSame = (str1 == str2);
System.out.println(areSame); // false

// Using equals()
boolean areEqual = str1.equals(str2);
System.out.println(areEqual); // true
```

The time complexity of the `==` operator is constant O(1) since it simply compares the memory addresses. The space complexity is also constant as no additional memory is used.

On the other hand, the time complexity of the `equals()` method is O(n), where n is the length of the string being compared. This is because the method needs to compare each character of the strings until a mismatch is found or the end of either string is reached. The space complexity is also O(1) since no additional memory is used.

When comparing strings of different lengths, the `equals()` method performs better than `==` as it only compares the characters until a mismatch is found or the shorter string ends. In contrast, the `==` operator will always return false if the strings have different lengths, regardless of their content. Therefore, comparing strings of different lengths with `equals()` is more efficient than using `==` since it avoids unnecessary character comparisons.

Increase in Difficulty:

1. A time complexity requirement for the implementation of `equals()` method could be O(1) if the strings have a predetermined fixed length and the comparison can be done in constant time without iterating over the characters. However, in the general case where the length of the strings is not known in advance, achieving a time complexity of O(1) is not possible due to the need to compare each character.

2. The `==` operator has a time complexity of O(1) and constant space complexity. The `equals()` method has a time complexity of O(n) and constant space complexity.

3. When comparing strings of different lengths, the performance impact depends on the lengths of the strings and their content. The `equals()` method will be more efficient as it stops comparing characters once a mismatch is found or the shorter string ends. However, if the strings have similar prefixes and the comparison is done character by character, there will be a larger performance impact due to the need to iterate over more characters. In such cases, using other techniques like hashing or sorting the strings before comparison might provide better performance.

