Here's the implementation of the Java program that finds the index of the first occurrence of a target element in a sorted array, even if the array contains duplicates.

```java
public class Main {
    public static int findFirstOccurrence(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;
        int result = -1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                result = mid;
                right = mid - 1; // Search in the left subarray for the first occurrence
            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {2, 4, 6, 6, 6, 8, 10};
        int target = 6;

        int index = findFirstOccurrence(arr, target);

        if (index == -1) {
            System.out.println("Element not found in the array");
        } else {
            System.out.println("First occurrence of the element is at index " + index);
        }
    }
}
```

The `findFirstOccurrence` method implements the binary search algorithm to find the first occurrence of the target element in the sorted array. It uses two pointers, `left` and `right`, to define the search range. The method continuously updates these pointers until the target element is found or the search range is exhausted.

The `main` method provides an example usage of the `findFirstOccurrence` method. In this example, we have an array `{2, 4, 6, 6, 6, 8, 10}` and we want to find the first occurrence of the target element `6`. The program outputs the index `2` since `6` is first found at index `2` in the array.

