Here is an example of how you can create a class in Java to store a list of students, their courses, and their corresponding grades. It also includes methods to calculate the average grade for each student and for each course.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class StudentGradeBook {
    private Map<String, Map<String, List<Integer>>> gradeBook;

    public StudentGradeBook() {
        gradeBook = new HashMap<>();
    }

    public void addStudent(String studentName) {
        gradeBook.put(studentName, new HashMap<>());
    }

    public void addCourse(String studentName, String courseName) {
        gradeBook.get(studentName).put(courseName, new ArrayList<>());
    }

    public void addGrade(String studentName, String courseName, int grade) {
        gradeBook.get(studentName).get(courseName).add(grade);
    }

    public double getAverageGradeForStudent(String studentName) {
        double sum = 0;
        int count = 0;
        for (Map.Entry<String, List<Integer>> entry : gradeBook.get(studentName).entrySet()) {
            for (int grade : entry.getValue()) {
                sum += grade;
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count > 0 ? sum / count : 0;
    }

    public double getAverageGradeForCourse(String courseName) {
        double sum = 0;
        int count = 0;
        for (Map<String, List<Integer>> studentGrades : gradeBook.values()) {
            if (studentGrades.containsKey(courseName)) {
                for (int grade : studentGrades.get(courseName)) {
                    sum += grade;
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }
        return count > 0 ? sum / count : 0;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StudentGradeBook gradeBook = new StudentGradeBook();

        // Add students
        gradeBook.addStudent("John");
        gradeBook.addStudent("Jane");

        // Add courses
        gradeBook.addCourse("John", "Math");
        gradeBook.addCourse("John", "Science");
        gradeBook.addCourse("Jane", "English");

        // Add grades
        gradeBook.addGrade("John", "Math", 90);
        gradeBook.addGrade("John", "Math", 95);
        gradeBook.addGrade("John", "Science", 80);
        gradeBook.addGrade("Jane", "English", 85);

        // Calculate average grades
        System.out.println("Average grade for John: " + gradeBook.getAverageGradeForStudent("John"));
        System.out.println("Average grade for Jane: " + gradeBook.getAverageGradeForStudent("Jane"));
        System.out.println("Average grade for Math: " + gradeBook.getAverageGradeForCourse("Math"));
        System.out.println("Average grade for Science: " + gradeBook.getAverageGradeForCourse("Science"));
        System.out.println("Average grade for English: " + gradeBook.getAverageGradeForCourse("English"));
    }
}
```

In this example, the `StudentGradeBook` class uses a `Map` to store the student names as keys and another `Map` as the corresponding value. This inner `Map` uses the course names as keys and a list of grades as the corresponding value.

The `addStudent`, `addCourse`, and `addGrade` methods are used to add students, courses, and grades respectively.

The `getAverageGradeForStudent` method calculates the average grade for a specific student by iterating over the courses and their corresponding grades and summing them up. It returns the average grade as a `double`.

The `getAverageGradeForCourse` method calculates the average grade for a specific course by iterating over all students and their corresponding grades for the course. It returns the average grade as a `double`.

In the `main` method, an instance of `StudentGradeBook` is created. Students, courses, and grades are added using the provided methods. Finally, the average grades for specific students and courses are calculated and printed to the console.

