Here's a C++ program that uses multi-threading to print all prime numbers between 1 and 1000. In this approach, we divide the range of numbers into multiple chunks and assign each chunk to a separate thread to check for primality.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

// Function to check if a number is prime
bool isPrime(int num) {
    if (num <= 1)
        return false;
    for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
        if (num % i == 0)
            return false;
    }
    return true;
}

// Function to check prime numbers within a range
void findPrimes(int start, int end) {
    for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
        if (isPrime(i)) {
            std::cout << i << " ";
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    const int THREAD_COUNT = 4; // Number of threads
    const int NUM_RANGE = 1000; // Range of numbers

    // Create a vector to store threads
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;

    // Calculate the chunk size for each thread
    int chunkSize = NUM_RANGE / THREAD_COUNT;

    // Create and start threads
    for (int i = 0; i < THREAD_COUNT; i++) {
        int start = i * chunkSize + 1;
        int end = (i == THREAD_COUNT - 1) ? NUM_RANGE : (i + 1) * chunkSize;
        threads.push_back(std::thread(findPrimes, start, end));
    }

    // Join threads
    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        thread.join();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program creates `THREAD_COUNT` number of threads to divide the range `1` to `1000` into chunks. Each thread checks the primality of numbers within its assigned chunk using the `isPrime` function. The prime numbers found by each thread are printed to the console. The program uses the `std::thread` library to handle multi-threading and `std::vector` to store the threads.

