{ 
    if (*head_ref == NULL) 
        return; 

    // Store head node 
    struct Node* temp = *head_ref; 

    // If header is removed 
    if (position == 0) 
    { 
        *head_ref = temp->next;   // Change head 
        free(temp);               // free old head 
        return; 
    } 

    // Find previous node of the node to be deleted 
    for (int i=0; temp!=NULL && i<position-1; i++) 
        temp = temp->next; 

    // If position is more than number of ndoes 
    if (temp == NULL || temp->next == NULL) 
        return; 

    // Node temp->next is the node to be deleted 
    // Store pointer to the next of node to be deleted 
    struct Node *next = temp->next->next; 

    // Unlink the node from linked list 
    free(temp->next);  // Free memory 

    temp->next = next;  // Unlink the deleted node from list 
}

Here's an example implementation of a Java program that displays prime numbers from 1 to 1000 in a tabular format, calculates the sum of all prime numbers, and utilizes multi-threading to enhance performance by calculating prime numbers in parallel:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PrimeNumbersTable {

    private static final int NUM_THREADS = 4; // Number of threads to use for parallel execution

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
        List<PrimeThread> threads = new ArrayList<>();

        // Create and start the threads
        for (int i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; i++) {
            PrimeThread thread = new PrimeThread(i + 1, NUM_THREADS, primes);
            threads.add(thread);
            thread.start();
        }

        // Wait for all threads to finish
        for (PrimeThread thread : threads) {
            thread.join();
        }

        // Display the prime numbers in a tabular format
        System.out.println("Prime Numbers from 1 to 1000:");
        System.out.println("-----------------------------");
        for (int i = 0; i < primes.size(); i++) {
            System.out.printf("%-5d", primes.get(i));
            if ((i + 1) % 10 == 0) {
                System.out.println();
            }
        }

        // Calculate and display the sum of all prime numbers
        int sum = primes.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();
        System.out.println("\nSum of all prime numbers: " + sum);
    }

    static class PrimeThread extends Thread {
        private final int threadId;
        private final int numThreads;
        private final List<Integer> primes;

        PrimeThread(int threadId, int numThreads, List<Integer> primes) {
            this.threadId = threadId;
            this.numThreads = numThreads;
            this.primes = primes;
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            for (int i = threadId; i <= 1000; i += numThreads) {
                if (isPrime(i)) {
                    synchronized (primes) {
                        primes.add(i);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        private boolean isPrime(int num) {
            if (num <= 1) {
                return false;
            }
            for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
                if (num % i == 0) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            return true;
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, the `PrimeNumbersTable` class is responsible for creating and starting the required number of `PrimeThread` instances (based on the `NUM_THREADS` constant) to calculate the prime numbers in parallel. The `PrimeThread` class extends the `Thread` class and overrides the `run()` method to calculate prime numbers within a specific range assigned to each thread. The calculated prime numbers are stored in the `primes` list, which is shared among all the threads.

After all the threads finish their execution, the program displays the prime numbers in a tabular format and calculates the sum of all prime numbers using the Java 8 stream API.

Note that this implementation may not provide significant performance improvements due to the small range of prime numbers (1 to 1000). However, the multi-threading approach can be beneficial for larger ranges or more computationally intensive tasks.

