- If we reach the end of `s2` and `index` is equal to the length of `s1`, it means that we have found all the characters of `s1` in the correct order in `s2`, so we return `true`. Otherwise, we return `false`.

This implementation has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of `s2`. It does not use any additional data structures or arrays. It also handles edge cases efficiently, such as empty strings or strings with repeating characters, as it only checks for character equality and does not rely on any specific order or frequency of characters. It can also handle large input strings efficiently as it only needs to iterate through each character once.

