```javascript
function filterFalsyValues(arr) {
  // Remove falsy values
  const filteredArr = arr.filter(value => {
    // Exclude null values and include only string, number, and boolean values
    if (value === null || typeof value === 'undefined' || isNaN(value)) {
      return false;
    }
    return true;
  });

  // Remove duplicates and sort in ascending order
  const finalArr = [...new Set(filteredArr)].sort((a, b) => a - b);

  return finalArr;
}
```

This function first uses the `filter()` method to remove falsy values from the array. It checks each value and excludes null values and any value that is undefined or NaN. Only string, number, and boolean values are included.

Next, the function uses a combination of the `Set` object and the spread operator (`...`) to remove duplicates from the `filteredArr`. The spread operator converts the `Set` object back into an array.

Finally, the function uses the `sort()` method to sort the `finalArr` in ascending order.

The time complexity of this function is O(n), where n is the size of the input array. The `filter()` method has a linear time complexity of O(n), and the `Set` object and `sort()` method also have linear time complexity.

