To improve the execution speed of the program by implementing a parallel processing approach using a thread pool, you can make the following changes:

1. Replace the vector of threads with a thread pool that limits the number of tasks to a maximum of 10 at any given time.

2. Use a condition variable to notify worker threads when new tasks are available in the task queue.

3. Modify the worker function to wait for tasks to become available in the queue before attempting to process them.

Here's the modified code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <functional>
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

int n = 100;
int numThreads = 4;
int maxTasks = 10;
std::mutex mtx;
std::condition_variable cv;
bool finished = false;

void worker(std::queue<int>& tasks)
{
    while (true)
    {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        // Wait for tasks to become available in the queue or for the program to finish
        cv.wait(lock, [&]{ return !tasks.empty() || finished; });

        // If no more tasks and the program has finished, exit the thread
        if (tasks.empty() && finished)
            break;

        // Process tasks
        while (!tasks.empty())
        {
            int task = tasks.front();
            tasks.pop();
            lock.unlock();  // Release the lock while processing the task
            std::cout << task << "\n";
            lock.lock();    // Reacquire the lock after processing the task
        }
    }
}

void foo(int n)
{
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    std::queue<int> tasks;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        tasks.push(i);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; i++)
    {
        threads.push_back(std::thread(worker, std::ref(tasks)));
    }

    {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        finished = true;  // Notify worker threads that the program has finished
    }
    cv.notify_all();     // Wake up all worker threads

    for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; i++)
    {
        threads[i].join();
    }
}

int main()
{
    foo(n);
    return 0;
}
```

This modified code uses a thread pool with a maximum of 10 tasks running in parallel at any given time. The worker threads wait for tasks to become available in the queue and exit gracefully when there are no more tasks and the program has finished. The condition variable and mutex are used to synchronize access to the task queue and to notify worker threads when new tasks are available or when the program has finished.

