Sure! Here's the Java code to calculate the sum of the first n Fibonacci numbers, where n is a prime number greater than 5:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class FibonacciSum {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a prime number greater than 5: ");
        int n = scanner.nextInt();

        if (isPrime(n) && n > 5) {
            int sum = calculateFibonacciSum(n);
            System.out.println("Sum of the first " + n + " Fibonacci numbers: " + sum);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a prime number greater than 5.");
        }
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int number) {
        if (number <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
            if (number % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static int calculateFibonacciSum(int n) {
        int sum = 0;
        int fib1 = 0;
        int fib2 = 1;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            sum += fib1;
            int nextFib = fib1 + fib2;
            fib1 = fib2;
            fib2 = nextFib;
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
```

