In C++, a constructor is a special member function of a class that is automatically called when an object of that class is created. It is used to initialize the object's data members and perform any necessary setup tasks. Constructors have the same name as the class and do not have a return type.

Here's an example of a class with a constructor that initializes an object with default values:

```cpp
class Person {
  private:
    std::string name;
    int age;

  public:
    Person() {
      name = "John Doe";
      age = 30;
    }
};
```

In this example, the `Person` class has a default constructor that initializes the `name` member with the value "John Doe" and the `age` member with the value 30. This constructor will be automatically called when a `Person` object is created without any arguments, like this:

```cpp
Person p;  // Calls the default constructor
```

The object `p` will now have the default values assigned by the constructor.

Constructors can also be defined to accept parameters, allowing objects to be initialized with user-defined values. Here's an example:

```cpp
class Person {
  private:
    std::string name;
    int age;

  public:
    Person(std::string n, int a) {
      name = n;
      age = a;
    }
};
```

In this case, the `Person` class has a constructor that takes two parameters: `n` for the name and `a` for the age. When a `Person` object is created with arguments, the corresponding constructor will be called and the values will be assigned to the respective data members:

```cpp
