```

The object `p` will now have the name "Jane Smith" and age 25.

Constructor overloading allows multiple constructors to be defined for a class with different parameter lists. This is useful when you want to provide flexibility in object initialization. Here's an example:

```cpp
class Person {
  private:
    std::string name;
    int age;

  public:
    Person() {
      name = "John Doe";
      age = 30;
    }

    Person(std::string n) {
      name = n;
      age = 0;
    }

    Person(std::string n, int a) {
      name = n;
      age = a;
    }
};
```

In this case, the `Person` class has three constructors: a default constructor, a constructor with only the name parameter, and a constructor with both the name and age parameters. Each constructor initializes the object with different default values. Now, you can create `Person` objects using any of these constructors:

```cpp
Person p1;            // Calls the default constructor
Person p2("Jane");    // Calls the constructor with only the name parameter
Person p3("Mike", 40);  // Calls the constructor with both name and age parameters
```

Constructors play a vital role in object-oriented programming. They allow objects to be initialized properly, ensuring that their data members are in a valid state. Constructors are used extensively in inheritance, where a derived class may have its own constructor that first calls the constructor of its base class to initialize the inherited members. This allows for the initialization of the entire object hierarchy.

Constructors are also crucial in polymorphism. When objects of derived classes are stored in base class pointers or references, the base class constructor is called when the object is created. However, since the derived class objects are being stored, their constructors are implicitly called to ensure proper initialization of their data members.

In summary, constructors in C++ are special member functions used to initialize objects. They can be used to set default values or accept parameters to initialize objects with user-defined values. Constructor overloading allows multiple constructors with different parameter lists to be defined. Constructors are essential in object-oriented programming for proper object initialization and are extensively used in inheritance and polymorphism.

