Here is a PHP function that reverses the order of an array without using the built-in array_reverse function, without using any additional arrays or variables to store intermediate values, and with a time complexity of O(n):

```php
function reverseArray(&$arr) {
  $length = count($arr);

  for ($i = 0; $i < $length / 2; $i++) {
    $temp = $arr[$i];
    $arr[$i] = $arr[$length - $i - 1];
    $arr[$length - $i - 1] = $temp;
  }
}
```

Explanation:

- The function takes the input array by reference using the `&` symbol in the function definition, so any changes made to the array within the function will be reflected outside of it as well.
- It calculates the length of the array using the `count` function.
- The function then loops through the first half of the array (up to `$length / 2`) and swaps each element with the corresponding element from the end of the array.
- The swapping is done using a temporary variable `$temp` to store the value of the current element being swapped.
- After the loop finishes, the array will be reversed in place.

