One efficient algorithm to search for a target element in a sorted array with a time complexity better than O(log n) is the Binary Jump Search algorithm. This algorithm is an optimized version of the Binary Search algorithm.

Here's the Java implementation of the Binary Jump Search algorithm:

```java
public class BinaryJumpSearch {
    public static int jumpSearch(int[] arr, int target) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int step = (int) Math.floor(Math.sqrt(n));
        int prev = 0;

        while (arr[Math.min(step, n) - 1] < target) {
            prev = step;
            step += (int) Math.floor(Math.sqrt(n));
            if (prev >= n) {
                return -1;
            }
        }

        while (arr[prev] < target) {
            prev++;
            if (prev == Math.min(step, n)) {
                return -1;
            }
        }

        if (arr[prev] == target) {
            return prev;
        }

        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9};
        int target = 5;
        int index = jumpSearch(arr, target);

        if (index != -1) {
            System.out.println("Target element found at index " + index);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Target element not found in the array");
        }
    }
}
```

In this algorithm, we use the idea of "jumping" through the array in fixed steps. Initially, the step size is the square root of the array size. We keep increasing the step size until we find a range where the target element may exist. Then, we perform a linear search within that range to find the target element.

The time complexity of the Binary Jump Search algorithm is approximately O(sqrt(n)) in the worst case. This is because the array is divided into sqrt(n) blocks, and each block is linearly searched if the target element is not found in the previous block.

Note: The given algorithm assumes the array is sorted in ascending order.

