Here's an example of how you could implement a table component in React that includes sortable columns, pagination, and filtering functionality:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const Table = ({ data }) => {
  const [currentPage, setCurrentPage] = useState(1);
  const [rowsPerPage] = useState(5);
  const [sortColumn, setSortColumn] = useState('');
  const [filterText, setFilterText] = useState('');

  // Get current rows
  const indexOfLastRow = currentPage * rowsPerPage;
  const indexOfFirstRow = indexOfLastRow - rowsPerPage;
  const currentRows = data.slice(indexOfFirstRow, indexOfLastRow);

  // Sort rows
  const sortedRows = sortColumn
    ? [...currentRows].sort((a, b) =>
        a[sortColumn].localeCompare(b[sortColumn])
      )
    : currentRows;

  // Filter rows
  const filteredRows = filterText
    ? sortedRows.filter(row =>
        Object.values(row).some(
          value =>
            value.toString().toLowerCase().indexOf(filterText.toLowerCase()) >
            -1
        )
      )
    : sortedRows;

  // Change page
  const paginate = pageNumber => setCurrentPage(pageNumber);

  // Handle column click
  const handleColumnClick = column => {
    if (sortColumn === column) {
      setSortColumn('');
    } else {
      setSortColumn(column);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="text"
        placeholder="Filter..."
        value={filterText}
        onChange={e => setFilterText(e.target.value)}
      />
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th onClick={() => handleColumnClick('name')}>
              Name
              {sortColumn === 'name' ? ' ▲' : ' ▼'}
            </th>
            <th onClick={() => handleColumnClick('age')}>
              Age
              {sortColumn === 'age' ? ' ▲' : ' ▼'}
            </th>
            <th onClick={() => handleColumnClick('location')}>
              Location
              {sortColumn === 'location' ? ' ▲' : ' ▼'}
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {filteredRows.map(row => (
            <tr key={row.id}>
              <td>{row.name}</td>
              <td>{row.age}</td>
              <td>{row.location}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <Pagination
        rowsPerPage={rowsPerPage}
        totalRows={data.length}
        currentPage={currentPage}
        paginate={paginate}
      />
    </div>
  );
};

const Pagination = ({ rowsPerPage, totalRows, currentPage, paginate }) => {
  const pageNumbers = [];

  for (let i = 1; i <= Math.ceil(totalRows / rowsPerPage); i++) {
    pageNumbers.push(i);
  }

  return (
    <div>
      {pageNumbers.map(number => (
        <button key={number} onClick={() => paginate(number)}>
          {number}
        </button>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Table;
```

In this example, the `Table` component takes in an array of `data` as a prop. The `data` array should contain objects with profile information (e.g., `name`, `age`, `location`). The component uses `useState` to manage the current page, number of rows per page, sort column, and filter text. It then calculates the current rows based on the current page and rows per page. The `sortedRows` and `filteredRows` variables are created based on the sort column and filter text, respectively.

The component renders an input element for filtering, a table header with sortable column headers, and a table body with the filtered and sorted rows. It also includes a `Pagination` component that handles pagination and renders page buttons based on the number of rows and rows per page.

The `handleColumnClick` function is responsible for toggling the sort order when a column header is clicked. It updates the `sortColumn` state accordingly. The sort order is indicated by the '▲' or '▼' arrow symbols appended to the column header.

You can then use this `Table` component in your application by passing the appropriate `data` prop containing the user's profile information.

