Here is a JavaScript program that checks if a string has at least two uppercase alphabets, two lowercase alphabets, two numeric characters, and two special characters:

```javascript
function checkStringValidity(str) {
  // Counters for each category
  let uppercaseCount = 0;
  let lowercaseCount = 0;
  let numericCount = 0;
  let specialCharCount = 0;

  // Regular expressions for each category
  const uppercaseRegex = /[A-Z]/;
  const lowercaseRegex = /[a-z]/;
  const numericRegex = /[0-9]/;
  const specialCharRegex = /[!@#$%^&*()_+\-=\[\]{};':"\\|,.<>\/?]/;

  // Iterate over each character in the string
  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
    const char = str[i];

    // Check if the character matches any category
    if (uppercaseRegex.test(char)) {
      uppercaseCount++;
    } else if (lowercaseRegex.test(char)) {
      lowercaseCount++;
    } else if (numericRegex.test(char)) {
      numericCount++;
    } else if (specialCharRegex.test(char)) {
      specialCharCount++;
    }
  }

  // Check if all categories have at least two characters
  return (
    uppercaseCount >= 2 &&
    lowercaseCount >= 2 &&
    numericCount >= 2 &&
    specialCharCount >= 2
  );
}

// Test the program
const testString = "AbC12@#$";
console.log(checkStringValidity(testString)); // Output: true
```

In this program, we define a function `checkStringValidity` that takes a string as input and returns `true` if the string meets the criteria (at least two uppercase letters, two lowercase letters, two numeric characters, and two special characters), otherwise it returns `false`.

We use regular expressions to check if each character matches the desired category. We initialize counters for each category and iterate over each character in the string, incrementing the appropriate counter if a match is found.

Finally, we check if all the counters have a count of at least two and return the result.

