Lazy loading is a design pattern in object-oriented programming where the initialization of an object or a resource is deferred until it is actually needed. This approach is used to improve performance and efficiency by avoiding unnecessary work upfront.

In lazy loading, the object or resource is only created or loaded when it is explicitly requested for the first time. This can be particularly useful when dealing with large or expensive objects that might not be needed immediately or at all.

Here's an example code snippet in Java that demonstrates the implementation of lazy loading:

```java
public class LazyLoadedObject {
    private ExpensiveObject expensiveObject;

    public ExpensiveObject getExpensiveObject() {
        if (expensiveObject == null) {
            expensiveObject = new ExpensiveObject();
        }
        return expensiveObject;
    }
}
```

In the above code, the `LazyLoadedObject` class has a private field `expensiveObject` which represents an expensive object that we want to lazily load. The `getExpensiveObject()` method is responsible for lazy loading the `expensiveObject`. It checks if the object has already been initialized. If not, it creates a new instance and assigns it to the field. Finally, it returns the object.

To incorporate multithreading for improved performance and efficiency, we can use synchronization and the double-check locking technique to ensure thread-safety. Here's an updated code snippet:

```java
public class LazyLoadedObject {
    private volatile ExpensiveObject expensiveObject;

    public ExpensiveObject getExpensiveObject() {
        if (expensiveObject == null) {
            synchronized (this) {
                if (expensiveObject == null) {
                    expensiveObject = new ExpensiveObject();
                }
            }
        }
        return expensiveObject;
    }
}
```

In the modified code, we have added the `volatile` keyword to the `expensiveObject` field to ensure that its value is always read from and written to the main memory, avoiding any thread-caching issues.

Additionally, we have added synchronization using the `synchronized` keyword to the critical section of code inside the `getExpensiveObject()` method. This ensures that only one thread can enter the block at a time, preventing multiple threads from creating separate instances of the `ExpensiveObject` class.

By incorporating multithreading with synchronization and double-check locking, we can achieve improved performance and efficiency while lazily loading the expensive object.

