The for loop, while loop, and do-while loop are control structures used to execute a block of code repeatedly until a certain condition is met. Here are the differences between them:

1. For loop:
   - The for loop is a compact loop structure that includes an initialization statement, a condition, and an increment statement all in one line.
   - It is commonly used when the number of iterations is known or when looping through elements in an array or collection.
   - Example:
     ```java
     for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
         System.out.println(i);
     }
     ```
   - Time complexity: O(n) - the time complexity increases linearly with the number of iterations.
   - Space complexity: O(1) - the space complexity remains constant as no additional space is required.

2. While loop:
   - The while loop executes a block of code repeatedly as long as the given condition is true.
   - It is commonly used when the number of iterations is not known in advance or when waiting for a condition to become false.
   - Example:
     ```java
     int i = 0;
     while (i < 10) {
         System.out.println(i);
         i++;
     }
     ```
   - Time complexity: O(n) - the time complexity increases linearly with the number of iterations.
   - Space complexity: O(1) - the space complexity remains constant as no additional space is required.

3. Do-while loop:
   - The do-while loop is similar to the while loop, but it executes the block of code at least once before checking the condition.
   - It is commonly used when you want to ensure that the code inside the loop is executed at least once.
   - Example:
     ```java
     int i = 0;
     do {
         System.out.println(i);
         i++;
     } while (i < 10);
     ```
   - Time complexity: O(n) - the time complexity increases linearly with the number of iterations.
   - Space complexity: O(1) - the space complexity remains constant as no additional space is required.

