Here is an example program that reads a dataset from a file and stores it in memory using a fixed amount of memory, while also handling memory leaks using a custom memory management technique:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <vector>

// Custom memory management technique using reference counting
template <typename T>
class ManagedMemory {
private:
    struct Reference {
        int count;
        T* ptr;
    };

    Reference* ref;

public:
    ManagedMemory() : ref(nullptr) {}

    explicit ManagedMemory(T* ptr) : ref(new Reference{1, ptr}) {}

    ManagedMemory(const ManagedMemory& other) : ref(other.ref) {
        if (ref) {
            ref->count++;
        }
    }

    ~ManagedMemory() {
        if (ref) {
            ref->count--;
            if (ref->count == 0) {
                delete ref->ptr;
                delete ref;
            }
        }
    }

    T* get() const {
        return ref ? ref->ptr : nullptr;
    }

    ManagedMemory& operator=(const ManagedMemory& other) {
        if (ref) {
            ref->count--;
            if (ref->count == 0) {
                delete ref->ptr;
                delete ref;
            }
        }

        ref = other.ref;
        if (ref) {
            ref->count++;
        }

        return *this;
    }
};

// Function to detect and fix memory leaks
template <typename T>
void detectAndFixLeaks(ManagedMemory<T>& managedMemory) {
    // If there is a reference but no active references, deallocate the memory
    if (managedMemory.get() && managedMemory.get()->count == 0) {
        delete managedMemory.get();
        managedMemory = nullptr;
    }
}

// Function to read dataset from file and store it in memory
void readDatasetFromFile(const std::string& filename, ManagedMemory<std::vector<int>>& managedMemory) {
    std::ifstream file(filename);
    if (!file) {
        std::cout << "Error opening file." << std::endl;
        return;
    }

    std::vector<int>* data = new std::vector<int>();

    int num;
    while (file >> num) {
        data->push_back(num);
    }

    managedMemory = ManagedMemory<std::vector<int>>(data);
}

int main() {
    ManagedMemory<std::vector<int>> managedMemory;

    // Read dataset from file and store it in memory
    readDatasetFromFile("dataset.txt", managedMemory);

    // Perform some operations on the dataset...

    // Detect and fix memory leaks
    detectAndFixLeaks(managedMemory);

    return 0;
}
```

In the above code, we use a custom memory management technique based on reference counting. The `ManagedMemory` class wraps a pointer to the data and keeps track of the number of active references to that data. When the reference count reaches zero, indicating that no references exist, the memory is deallocated.

The `detectAndFixLeaks` function checks if there is a reference to the memory but no active references, indicating a memory leak. In this case, it deallocates the memory and sets the reference to `nullptr`.

Now, let's consider a potential erroneous code that could lead to memory leaks. Suppose we have the following code snippet:

```cpp
ManagedMemory<std::vector<int>> managedMemory;

// Read dataset from file and store it in memory
readDatasetFromFile("dataset.txt", managedMemory);

// Memory leak: forget to call detectAndFixLeaks(managedMemory);

// Perform some operations on the dataset...

return 0;
```

In this code, we forgot to call `detectAndFixLeaks(managedMemory)` after performing operations on the dataset. This would result in a memory leak, as the memory allocated for the dataset would not be deallocated.

To fix this memory leak, we need to call `detectAndFixLeaks(managedMemory)` before exiting the program. The `detectAndFixLeaks` function will identify the memory leak by checking if there is a reference to the memory but no active references. It will then deallocate the memory and set the reference to `nullptr`, preventing the memory leak from occurring.

