Here's an example code that implements Selection Sort recursively in Java:

```java
public class SelectionSort {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {5, 3, 7, 2, 8, 1, 6, 4, 9, 0};
        selectionSort(arr, 0);

        System.out.println("Sorted array:");
        for (int num : arr) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }

    public static void selectionSort(int[] arr, int startIndex) {
        if (startIndex >= arr.length - 1) {
            return;
        }

        int minIndex = findMinIndex(arr, startIndex, startIndex);

        // Swap the minimum element with the current element
        int temp = arr[startIndex];
        arr[startIndex] = arr[minIndex];
        arr[minIndex] = temp;

        // Recursively sort the remaining array
        selectionSort(arr, startIndex + 1);
    }

    public static int findMinIndex(int[] arr, int startIndex, int minIndex) {
        if (startIndex >= arr.length) {
            return minIndex;
        }

        if (arr[startIndex] < arr[minIndex]) {
            minIndex = startIndex;
        }

        return findMinIndex(arr, startIndex + 1, minIndex);
    }
}
```

This code starts by calling the `selectionSort` method with the input array and the starting index 0. The `selectionSort` method first checks if the starting index is already at the last element of the array. If it is, the method simply returns and the sorting process ends. Otherwise, it finds the index of the minimum element from the current index onwards using the `findMinIndex` method. It then swaps the minimum element with the current element at the starting index. Finally, it recursively calls the `selectionSort` method with the incremented starting index to sort the remaining part of the array.

The `findMinIndex` method is a helper method that recursively finds the index of the minimum element in the array starting from a given index. It checks if the given index is already beyond the array length and if so, returns the minimum index found so far. Otherwise, it compares the element at the current index with the element at the minimum index found so far and updates the minimum index if necessary. It then recursively calls itself with the incremented index and the updated minimum index.

