To convert a doubly linked list to an array using an iterative approach, we can traverse the list from the head node to the tail node, storing the values of each node in an array. Then, we can traverse the list from the tail node to the head node, appending the values of each node to the array.

Here is the implementation of the `convertToList` function:

```javascript
function convertToList(linkedList) {
  let currentNode = linkedList.head;
  const array = [];

  // Traverse from head to tail
  while (currentNode !== null) {
    array.push(currentNode.value);
    currentNode = currentNode.next;
  }

  // Traverse from tail to head
  currentNode = linkedList.tail.prev;
  while (currentNode !== null) {
    array.push(currentNode.value);
    currentNode = currentNode.prev;
  }

  return array;
}
```

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the linked list. We traverse the list twice: once from head to tail, and once from tail to head.

The space complexity of this solution is also O(n), as we store the values of the nodes in an array.

