Here's an implementation of the recursive binary search algorithm in Python:

def binary_search(arr, target):
    if len(arr) == 0:
        return False
    else:
        mid = len(arr) // 2
        if arr[mid] == target:
            return True
        elif arr[mid] < target:
            return binary_search(arr[mid+1:], target)
        else:
            return binary_search(arr[:mid], target)

# Example usage
arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]
target = 5
print(binary_search(arr, target))

In this implementation, the binary_search function takes an array (arr) and a target element (target) as input. If the array is empty, it returns False because the target cannot be found. Otherwise, it calculates the middle index (mid) of the array and compares the element at that index with the target. If they are equal, it returns True. If the element at mid is less than the target, it recursively calls the binary_search function on the right half of the array (arr[mid+1:]). If the element at mid is greater than the target, it recursively calls the binary_search function on the left half of the array (arr[:mid]). This process continues until the target is found or the array is empty.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log n) because the size of the array is halved in each recursive call. The space complexity is also O(log n) because the maximum depth of the recursive call stack is log n.

