To achieve a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(1), we can generate random numbers between 0 and 100, and continuously adjust them to ensure that the sum of all the elements in the array is divisible by 5.

Here's the C++ code for the required function:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <ctime>

int* generateArray(int n) {
    int* arr = new int[n];
    int sum = 0;

    // Generate random numbers between 0 and 100
    srand(time(0));
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        arr[i] = rand() % 101;
        sum += arr[i];
    }

    // Adjust the numbers to ensure the sum is divisible by 5
    int remainder = sum % 5;
    if (remainder != 0) {
        arr[n - 1] += (5 - remainder);
        sum += (5 - remainder);
    }

    std::cout << "Generated Array: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    std::cout << "Sum: " << sum << std::endl;

    return arr;
}

int main() {
    int n;
    std::cout << "Enter the size of the array: ";
    std::cin >> n;

    int* arr = generateArray(n);

    delete[] arr;
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the `generateArray` function takes an integer `n` as input and returns a dynamically allocated array of size `n`. It first initializes the array with random numbers between 0 and 100 and keeps track of their sum. It then calculates the remainder when dividing the sum by 5. If the remainder is non-zero, it adjusts the last element of the array to make the sum divisible by 5.

The main function prompts the user to enter the size of the array and calls the `generateArray` function to generate and print the resulting array. Finally, it deletes the dynamically allocated array to free the memory.

