Increase the complexity further by adding multiple producers and consumers to the producer-consumer scenario. Modify the code to implement a bounded buffer with multiple producers and consumers using mutex locks and condition variables.

```cpp
#include <pthread.h>

#define BUFFER_SIZE 10
#define NUM_PRODUCERS 3
#define NUM_CONSUMERS 2

int buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
int count = 0;
int in = 0;
int out = 0;

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_cond_t full = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;
pthread_cond_t empty = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;

void* producer_func(void* arg) {
    int data = *(int*)arg;
    while (1) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
        while (count == BUFFER_SIZE) {
            pthread_cond_wait(&full, &mutex);
        }

        buffer[in] = data;
        in = (in + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE;
        count++;

        pthread_cond_signal(&empty);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

        data += NUM_PRODUCERS; // Increase data by NUM_PRODUCERS to avoid duplicates
    }
    return NULL;
}

void* consumer_func(void* arg) {
    while (1) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
        while (count == 0) {
            pthread_cond_wait(&empty, &mutex);
        }

        int data = buffer[out];
        out = (out + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE;
        count--;

        pthread_cond_signal(&full);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

        // Process the data

        printf("Consumed by Thread %d: %d\n", *(int*)arg, data);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t producer_threads[NUM_PRODUCERS], consumer_threads[NUM_CONSUMERS];
    int producer_ids[NUM_PRODUCERS], consumer_ids[NUM_CONSUMERS];

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_PRODUCERS; i++) {
        producer_ids[i] = i;
        pthread_create(&producer_threads[i], NULL, producer_func, (void*)&producer_ids[i]);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_CONSUMERS; i++) {
        consumer_ids[i] = i;
        pthread_create(&consumer_threads[i], NULL, consumer_func, (void*)&consumer_ids[i]);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_PRODUCERS; i++) {
        pthread_join(producer_threads[i], NULL);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_CONSUMERS; i++) {
        pthread_join(consumer_threads[i], NULL);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this further modified test, the code now implements a bounded buffer with multiple producers and consumers. The `producer_func` function is executed by multiple producer threads, each generating data and adding it to the buffer. The `consumer_func` function is executed by multiple consumer threads, each consuming data from the buffer. 

To ensure thread-safety, mutex locks and condition variables are used as before. However, additional complexity arises from coordinating the multiple producers and consumers. Each producer thread generates unique data by increasing the value of `data` by `NUM_PRODUCERS`. This ensures that the produced data is distinct and avoids duplicates.

The `consumer_func` function also includes the ID of the consumer thread in the `printf` statement to track which consumer consumes each data item.

Overall, this further modified test requires a deeper understanding of synchronization mechanisms and demonstrates the ability to handle more complex concurrency scenarios with multiple producers and consumers.

