One way to sort the array in descending order without using any temporary arrays or data structures is by using the bubble sort algorithm.

Here is an example implementation in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < arr[j + 1]) {
                // Swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {4, 2, 9, 5, 1, 3};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    bubbleSort(arr, n);

    std::cout << "Sorted array in descending order: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Sorted array in descending order: 9 5 4 3 2 1
```

In this implementation, the `bubbleSort` function uses two nested loops to iterate over the array and compare adjacent elements. If the current element is smaller than the next element, they are swapped. This process is repeated until the array is sorted in descending order. The original array is modified in-place.

Here is an example of a stored procedure in SQL that meets your requirements:

```sql
-- Create the table for storing successful and failed insertions
CREATE TABLE InsertionLog (
    LogId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    TableName VARCHAR(100),
    InsertedData VARCHAR(MAX),
    ErrorMessage VARCHAR(MAX),
    InsertionTime DATETIME
);

-- Create the stored procedure
CREATE PROCEDURE InsertStudent
    @Name VARCHAR(100),
    @Age INT,
    @Gender VARCHAR(10),
    @ErrorMessage VARCHAR(MAX) OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;

    -- Declare variables for concurrency control
    DECLARE @RetryCount INT = 0;
    DECLARE @MaxRetries INT = 5;
    DECLARE @Success BIT = 0;

    -- Begin transaction for data consistency
    BEGIN TRANSACTION;

    -- Handle concurrency control using a loop
    WHILE @RetryCount <= @MaxRetries AND @Success = 0
    BEGIN
        BEGIN TRY
            -- Insert records into the Students table
            INSERT INTO Students (Name, Age, Gender)
            VALUES (@Name, @Age, @Gender);

            -- Commit transaction
            COMMIT;

            -- Set success flag to true
            SET @Success = 1;

            -- Log successful insertion
            INSERT INTO InsertionLog (LogId, TableName, InsertedData, InsertionTime)
            VALUES (NEWID(), 'Students', CONCAT('Name: ', @Name, ', Age: ', CAST(@Age AS VARCHAR(10)), ', Gender: ', @Gender), GETDATE());

        END TRY
        BEGIN CATCH
            -- Rollback transaction
            ROLLBACK;

            -- Log failed insertion
            INSERT INTO InsertionLog (LogId, TableName, InsertedData, ErrorMessage, InsertionTime)
            VALUES (NEWID(), 'Students', CONCAT('Name: ', @Name, ', Age: ', CAST(@Age AS VARCHAR(10)), ', Gender: ', @Gender), ERROR_MESSAGE(), GETDATE());

            -- Set error message output parameter
            SET @ErrorMessage = ERROR_MESSAGE();

            -- Increment retry count
            SET @RetryCount = @RetryCount + 1;
        END CATCH;
    END;

    -- Check if the maximum number of retries has been reached
    IF @RetryCount > @MaxRetries
    BEGIN
        SET @ErrorMessage = 'Failed to insert record after maximum retry attempts.';
    END;

END;
```

This stored procedure handles concurrent insertions by using a loop with a retry count. If a concurrent insertion occurs and causes a violation of unique constraints, the loop will retry a certain number of times (specified by `@MaxRetries`) before giving up.

It also logs all successful and failed insertions in the `InsertionLog` table, which includes the table name, inserted data, error message (if applicable), and insertion time.

