Here's a Java function that finds the largest prime number within a given range of numbers and also returns the number of prime numbers found within that range:

```java
public class PrimeNumberFinder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int start = 1;
        int end = 100;

        int largestPrime = findLargestPrime(start, end);
        int primeCount = countPrimes(start, end);

        System.out.println("Largest prime number: " + largestPrime);
        System.out.println("Number of prime numbers found: " + primeCount);
    }

    public static int findLargestPrime(int start, int end) {
        int largestPrime = 0;

        for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            if (isPrime(i)) {
                largestPrime = i;
            }
        }

        return largestPrime;
    }

    public static int countPrimes(int start, int end) {
        int primeCount = 0;

        for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            if (isPrime(i)) {
                primeCount++;
            }
        }

        return primeCount;
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int number) {
        if (number <= 1) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
            if (number % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

In this example, the `findLargestPrime` function iterates through the given range of numbers and checks if each number is prime using the `isPrime` function. It updates the `largestPrime` variable with the largest prime number found within the range.

The `countPrimes` function also iterates through the range and increments the `primeCount` variable each time a prime number is found.

