        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        double number = scanner.nextDouble();

        int sum = sumOfDigits(number);
        System.out.println("Sum of digits: " + sum);
    }

    public static int sumOfDigits(double number) {
        int sum = 0;

        // Convert the number to a string for easier digit extraction
        String numberString = Double.toString(number);

        // Handle negative numbers
        if (numberString.startsWith("-")) {
            numberString = numberString.substring(1);
        }

        // Iterate over each character in the string
        for (char c : numberString.toCharArray()) {
            // Check if the character is a digit
            if (Character.isDigit(c)) {
                // Convert the character to integer and add it to the sum
                sum += Character.getNumericValue(c);
            }
        }

        // Handle negative numbers
        if (number < 0) {
            sum *= -1;
        }

        return sum;
    }
}
```

In this program, we first prompt the user to enter a number using the `Scanner` class. The `sumOfDigits` method is then called to calculate the sum of the digits. 

The `sumOfDigits` method takes a `double` number as input. It converts the number to a string and handles negative numbers by removing the "-" sign from the string representation. It then iterates over each character in the string and checks if it is a digit. If it is, the character is converted to an integer using `Character.getNumericValue(c)` and added to the sum. Finally, if the original number was negative, the sum is multiplied by -1 to maintain the correct sign.

After the calculation is done, the result is printed out in the `main` method.

