Here is a Java program that reads in two strings and outputs their longest common substring along with the starting and ending indices in each input string:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class LongestCommonSubstring {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter the first string: ");
        String str1 = scanner.nextLine();

        System.out.print("Enter the second string: ");
        String str2 = scanner.nextLine();

        String longestCommonSubstring = getLongestCommonSubstring(str1, str2);

        if (longestCommonSubstring.length() == 0) {
            System.out.println("No common substring found.");
        } else {
            int startIndex1 = str1.indexOf(longestCommonSubstring);
            int endIndex1 = startIndex1 + longestCommonSubstring.length() - 1;

            int startIndex2 = str2.indexOf(longestCommonSubstring);
            int endIndex2 = startIndex2 + longestCommonSubstring.length() - 1;

            System.out.println("Longest common substring: " + longestCommonSubstring);
            System.out.println("Starting index in the first string: " + startIndex1);
            System.out.println("Ending index in the first string: " + endIndex1);
            System.out.println("Starting index in the second string: " + startIndex2);
            System.out.println("Ending index in the second string: " + endIndex2);
        }
    }

    private static String getLongestCommonSubstring(String str1, String str2) {
        int[][] dp = new int[str1.length() + 1][str2.length() + 1];
        int maxLength = 0;
        int endIndex = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= str1.length(); i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= str2.length(); j++) {
                if (str1.charAt(i - 1) == str2.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;

                    if (dp[i][j] > maxLength) {
                        maxLength = dp[i][j];
                        endIndex = i - 1;
                    }
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = 0;
                }
            }
        }

        if (maxLength == 0) {
            return "";
        }

        return str1.substring(endIndex - maxLength + 1, endIndex + 1);
    }
}
```

This program uses a dynamic programming approach to find the longest common substring between the two input strings. The `getLongestCommonSubstring` method takes two strings as input and returns the longest common substring. It uses a two-dimensional array `dp` to store the lengths of common substrings at each position. The maximum length and the ending index of the longest common substring are updated during the traversal of the arrays. Finally, the `substring` method is used to extract the longest common substring from the first input string.

