Here's a C++ program that takes a string as input, ignores whitespace characters, and returns the most frequently occurring character(s):

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>

std::vector<char> findMostFrequentChar(const std::string& input) {
    std::unordered_map<char, int> charCount;
    int maxCount = 0;

    for (char c : input) {
        if (std::isalpha(c)) {  // Check if character is alphabetical
            charCount[c]++;
            maxCount = std::max(maxCount, charCount[c]);
        }
    }

    std::vector<char> mostFrequentChars;
    for (const auto& pair : charCount) {
        if (pair.second == maxCount) {
            mostFrequentChars.push_back(pair.first);
        }
    }

    return mostFrequentChars;
}

int main() {
    std::string input;
    std::cout << "Enter a string: ";
    std::getline(std::cin, input);

    std::vector<char> mostFrequentChars = findMostFrequentChar(input);

    if (mostFrequentChars.empty()) {
        std::cout << "No alphabetical characters found.\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "Most frequent character(s): ";
        for (char c : mostFrequentChars) {
            std::cout << c << " ";
        }
        std::cout << "\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we use an `unordered_map` to store the count of each alphabetical character encountered. We also keep track of the maximum count (`maxCount`) encountered so far.

We iterate over each character in the input string, and if it is an alphabetical character, we increment its count in the `charCount` map and update `maxCount` if necessary.

After counting the occurrences, we iterate over the `charCount` map to find all characters that have the same highest frequency (`maxCount`), and add them to the `mostFrequentChars` vector.

Finally, we output the most frequent character(s) or a message if no alphabetical characters were found.

