Here's an example of a JavaFX program that allows a user to move a square shape in 3 different directions using the arrow keys, and implements collision detection to prevent the square from overlapping with any other shapes on the screen:

```java
import javafx.application.Application;
import javafx.geometry.Bounds;
import javafx.scene.Scene;
import javafx.scene.input.KeyCode;
import javafx.scene.layout.Pane;
import javafx.scene.paint.Color;
import javafx.scene.shape.Rectangle;
import javafx.stage.Stage;

public class SquareMovement extends Application {
    private Rectangle square;
    private Pane pane;

    @Override
    public void start(Stage primaryStage) {
        pane = new Pane();
        Scene scene = new Scene(pane, 400, 400);

        square = new Rectangle(50, 50, Color.BLUE);
        square.setTranslateX(175);
        square.setTranslateY(175);

        pane.getChildren().add(square);

        scene.setOnKeyPressed(event -> {
            KeyCode keyCode = event.getCode();
            moveSquare(keyCode);
        });

        primaryStage.setTitle("Square Movement");
        primaryStage.setScene(scene);
        primaryStage.show();
    }

    private void moveSquare(KeyCode keyCode) {
        Bounds bounds = pane.getBoundsInLocal();
        switch (keyCode) {
            case UP:
                if (square.getTranslateY() - 10 >= bounds.getMinY()) {
                    square.setTranslateY(square.getTranslateY() - 10);
                }
                break;
            case DOWN:
                if (square.getTranslateY() + 10 <= bounds.getMaxY() - square.getHeight()) {
                    square.setTranslateY(square.getTranslateY() + 10);
                }
                break;
            case LEFT:
                if (square.getTranslateX() - 10 >= bounds.getMinX()) {
                    square.setTranslateX(square.getTranslateX() - 10);
                }
                break;
            case RIGHT:
                if (square.getTranslateX() + 10 <= bounds.getMaxX() - square.getWidth()) {
                    square.setTranslateX(square.getTranslateX() + 10);
                }
                break;
        }

        if (checkCollision()) {
            square.setFill(Color.RED);
        } else {
            square.setFill(Color.BLUE);
        }
    }

    private boolean checkCollision() {
        for (Node node : pane.getChildren()) {
            if (node != square && node.getBoundsInParent().intersects(square.getBoundsInParent())) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        launch(args);
    }
}
```

In this program, we create a `Rectangle` object representing the square shape and add it to a `Pane` object. The `Pane` is then added to the `Scene` of the `Stage`. We listen for key events on the scene and move the square accordingly using the `setTranslateX` and `setTranslateY` methods. We also check for collision with other shapes by comparing the bounds of the square with the bounds of other shapes using the `intersects` method. If a collision is detected, we change the color of the square to red.

