Here's an efficient Java program that uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm and multithreading to efficiently find the Nth prime number:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class NthPrimeNumber {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 100000; // Number of prime numbers to find

        List<Integer> primes = findPrimes(n);
        int nthPrime = primes.get(n - 1);
        System.out.println("The " + n + "th prime number is: " + nthPrime);
    }

    public static List<Integer> findPrimes(int n) {
        int sqrtN = (int) Math.sqrt(n);
        boolean[] isComposite = new boolean[n + 1];

        // Multithreading setup
        int numThreads = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();
        PrimeNumberThread[] threads = new PrimeNumberThread[numThreads];
        for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; i++) {
            threads[i] = new PrimeNumberThread(isComposite, n, i, numThreads);
            threads[i].start();
        }

        try {
            for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; i++) {
                threads[i].join();
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            if (!isComposite[i]) {
                primes.add(i);
            }
        }

        return primes;
    }

    static class PrimeNumberThread extends Thread {
        private boolean[] isComposite;
        private int n;
        private int start;
        private int step;

        public PrimeNumberThread(boolean[] isComposite, int n, int start, int step) {
            this.isComposite = isComposite;
            this.n = n;
            this.start = start;
            this.step = step;
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            int sqrtN = (int) Math.sqrt(n);
            for (int i = 2 + start; i <= sqrtN; i += step) {
                if (!isComposite[i]) {
                    for (int j = i * i; j <= n; j += i) {
                        isComposite[j] = true;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, we first calculate the square root of N and create a boolean array `isComposite` of size N + 1. Each index in the array represents a number, and if the value at that index is `true`, it means that the number is composite (i.e., not prime).

We then use multithreading to divide the work among multiple threads. Each thread is responsible for marking the multiples of a specific prime number as composite. The number of threads is determined by the available processors on the system.

After all the threads finish their work, we iterate through the `isComposite` array and add the prime numbers to a `List<Integer>`.

Finally, we retrieve the Nth prime number from the list and print it. The time complexity of this program is O(sqrt(N) * log(log(N))) as required.

Note: The program may take longer to run for larger values of N due to the memory usage and thread synchronization overhead. To further optimize the program, you can consider using segmented sieves or other advanced techniques.

