Here is a C++ program that calculates the mean, median, and mode of an array of integer numbers:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <map>

double calculateMean(const std::vector<int>& numbers) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int number : numbers) {
        sum += number;
    }
    return static_cast<double>(sum) / numbers.size();
}

double calculateMedian(const std::vector<int>& numbers) {
    std::vector<int> sortedNumbers = numbers;
    std::sort(sortedNumbers.begin(), sortedNumbers.end());
    int size = sortedNumbers.size();
    if (size % 2 == 0) {
        return (sortedNumbers[size / 2 - 1] + sortedNumbers[size / 2]) / 2.0;
    } else {
        return sortedNumbers[size / 2];
    }
}

std::vector<int> calculateMode(const std::vector<int>& numbers) {
    std::map<int, int> frequencyMap;
    int maxFrequency = 0;
    for (int number : numbers) {
        frequencyMap[number]++;
        maxFrequency = std::max(maxFrequency, frequencyMap[number]);
    }
    std::vector<int> modes;
    for (const auto& pair : frequencyMap) {
        if (pair.second == maxFrequency) {
            modes.push_back(pair.first);
        }
    }
    return modes;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 3, 5, 5, 1};

    double mean = calculateMean(numbers);
    double median = calculateMedian(numbers);
    std::vector<int> modes = calculateMode(numbers);

    std::cout << "Mean: " << mean << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Median: " << median << std::endl;
    if (modes.size() == 1) {
        std::cout << "Mode: " << modes[0] << std::endl;
    } else if (modes.size() > 1) {
        std::cout << "Modes: ";
        for (int mode : modes) {
            std::cout << mode << " ";
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "No mode" << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program uses three separate functions to calculate the mean, median, and mode of the input array.

The `calculateMean` function calculates the sum of all the numbers in the array and divides it by the total number of elements to find the mean.

The `calculateMedian` function first sorts the array in ascending order. Then, if the array has an odd number of elements, it returns the value at the center position. If the array has an even number of elements, it calculates the average of the two middle values.

The `calculateMode` function uses a map to store the frequency of each number in the array. It keeps track of the maximum frequency and finds all the numbers that have this frequency. The function returns a vector of modes, which can be empty if there is no mode or contain multiple modes if there are ties.

In the `main` function, an example array `[1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 3, 5, 5, 1]` is used to test the program. The calculated mean, median, and mode(s) are then printed to the console.

Note that in this program, we assume that the input array is non-empty. You may want to add additional input validation to handle empty arrays or handle such cases in a way that makes sense for your specific application.

