To efficiently calculate the median of a given list of integers without using any additional data structures, we can make use of the quickselect algorithm, which is a randomized algorithm for finding the kth smallest element in an unsorted list.

The idea behind the algorithm is to select a pivot element from the list, partition the list into two parts based on the pivot, and recursively apply the algorithm to the appropriate part until the desired element is found. In this case, we want to find the median, which is equivalent to finding the (n/2)th element in the sorted list, where n is the length of the list.

Here is the implementation of the `findMedian` function in Java:

```java
public static double findMedian(int[] nums) {
    int n = nums.length;
    int k = (n + 1) / 2; // k is the index of the median element

    int left = 0;
    int right = n - 1;

    while (left < right) {
        int pivotIndex = partition(nums, left, right);

        if (pivotIndex < k - 1) {
            left = pivotIndex + 1;
        } else if (pivotIndex > k - 1) {
            right = pivotIndex - 1;
        } else {
            break;
        }
    }

    if (n % 2 == 0) {
        // Average of two middle elements
        int median1 = nums[k - 1];
        int median2 = nums[k];
        return (median1 + median2) / 2.0;
    } else {
        return nums[k - 1];
    }
}

private static int partition(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
    int pivotIndex = left + (int) (Math.random() * (right - left + 1));
    int pivotValue = nums[pivotIndex];

    // Move pivot to the rightmost position
    swap(nums, pivotIndex, right);

    int storeIndex = left;
    for (int i = left; i < right; i++) {
        if (nums[i] < pivotValue) {
            swap(nums, i, storeIndex);
            storeIndex++;
        }
    }

    // Move pivot to its final sorted position
    swap(nums, storeIndex, right);

    return storeIndex;
}

private static void swap(int[] nums, int i, int j) {
    int temp = nums[i];
    nums[i] = nums[j];
    nums[j] = temp;
}
```

This implementation first calculates the index `k` of the median element. Then, it repeatedly partitions the list around a randomly selected pivot until the pivot index is equal to `k-1`. At that point, the median element has been found.

If the length of the list is even, we return the average of the `k-1` and `k` elements. Otherwise, we return the `k-1` element as the median.

The `partition` function is a helper function that partitions the list into two parts, with elements less than the pivot value on the left side and elements greater than or equal to the pivot value on the right side.

This algorithm has an average time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the list. It is efficient and can handle large numbers of elements without using any additional data structures.

