```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "Hello, World!";
        String reversed = reverseString(str);
        System.out.println(reversed);
    }

    public static String reverseString(String str) {
        // Check if the input string is within the length limits
        if (str.length() < 10 || str.length() > 100) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input string length should be between 10 and 100 characters.");
        }

        // Convert the string to a character array
        char[] charArray = str.toCharArray();

        // Reverse the character array
        int start = 0;
        int end = charArray.length - 1;
        while (start < end) {
            char temp = charArray[start];
            charArray[start] = charArray[end];
            charArray[end] = temp;
            start++;
            end--;
        }

        // Capitalize the first letter and convert the rest of the letters to lowercase
        charArray[0] = Character.toUpperCase(charArray[0]);
        for (int i = 1; i < charArray.length; i++) {
            charArray[i] = Character.toLowerCase(charArray[i]);
        }

        // Convert the character array back to a string and return it
        return new String(charArray);
    }
}
```

This implementation first checks if the input string length is within the required limits. If not, it throws an `IllegalArgumentException`.

Next, it converts the string to a character array and reverses it using a two-pointer approach. The `start` pointer starts at the beginning of the array, and the `end` pointer starts at the end of the array. The method swaps the characters at the `start` and `end` indices, and then moves the `start` pointer forward and the `end` pointer backward until they meet in the middle.

After reversing the character array, the method capitalizes the first letter using the `Character.toUpperCase` method, and converts the rest of the letters to lowercase using the `Character.toLowerCase` method.

Finally, the method converts the character array back to a string using the `new String` constructor and returns the reversed string.

