```java
public class ReverseString {

    public static String reverse(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            return input;
        }

        char[] characters = input.toCharArray();
        int start = 0;
        int end = characters.length - 1;

        while (start < end) {
            swap(characters, start, end);
            start++;
            end--;
        }

        return new String(characters);
    }

    private static void swap(char[] characters, int i, int j) {
        char temp = characters[i];
        characters[i] = characters[j];
        characters[j] = temp;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "Hello, World!";
        String reversed = reverse(input);
        System.out.println(reversed);
    }
}
```

The `reverse()` method takes an input string and converts it to a character array. It then uses a two-pointer technique to swap the characters from the start and end of the array, moving towards the center. This effectively reverses the string.

The `swap()` method is a helper function that swaps two characters in the array.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n) because it iterates over the string once. The space complexity is O(1) because it uses a constant amount of extra space to store the characters array and a few variables.

This algorithm can handle strings containing Unicode characters because it operates on the individual characters and does not rely on any built-in string manipulation methods. It maintains the correct character encoding because it simply swaps the characters in the array, preserving their original values.

To handle strings with a maximum length of 100 million characters efficiently, the algorithm uses a character array instead of creating a new string object at each step. This reduces memory usage and improves performance. The algorithm also ensures that it does not exceed a memory usage of 1 GB by using a fixed-size character array and a few variables, which take up a constant amount of memory regardless of the input size.

