import java.util.HashSet;

public class LongestSubstring {
    public static int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int n = s.length();
        int maxLen = 0;
        int left = 0;
        int right = 0;

        HashSet<Character> set = new HashSet<>();

        while (right < n) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            while (set.contains(c)) {
                set.remove(s.charAt(left));
                left++;
            }
            set.add(c);
            maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1);
            right++;
        }

        return maxLen;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "🐱🐮🐶🐱";
        int result = lengthOfLongestSubstring(input);
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}
```

Explanation:
- The function `lengthOfLongestSubstring` takes in a string `s` as a parameter and returns the length of the longest substring without repeating characters.
- It first checks if the string is empty or null, and if so, returns 0.
- It initializes variables `n` (the length of the string), `maxLen` (the maximum length of the substring), `left` (the left pointer of the substring), `right` (the right pointer of the substring), and a HashSet `set` to keep track of the characters in the current substring.
- It iterates through the string using the `right` pointer and checks each character.
- If the character is already in the `set`, it means there is a repeating character. In this case, it removes characters from the `set` starting from the `left` pointer until the repeating character is no longer in the `set`. It then increments the `left` pointer.
- After removing the repeating characters, it adds the current character to the `set` and updates the `maxLen` if necessary.
- Finally, it increments the `right` pointer and continues the loop until the end of the string is reached.
- The function returns the `maxLen` as the result.

