The Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) approach offers several benefits in programming, making it a popular choice for software development. Some of these benefits include code reusability, encapsulation, and modularity. Additionally, OOP promotes a clearer and more organized code structure, making it easier to understand, maintain, and collaborate on complex projects.

Here are three commonly used design patterns in OOP, along with their corresponding benefits:

1. Singleton Pattern:
The Singleton pattern ensures that only one instance of a class can be created, providing a global point of access to it. This can be beneficial in situations where there should be a single shared resource or when it is crucial to limit the instantiation of a class to a single object. For example, a logger class that tracks and records system activities throughout an application can be implemented using the Singleton pattern.

Erroneous code attempting to implement the Singleton pattern:
```java
public class Logger {
    private static Logger instance;

    private Logger() {
        // Constructor implementation
    }

    public static Logger getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Logger(); // Creating a new instance every time
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```
In this example, the code fails to adhere to the Singleton pattern because it creates a new instance of the Logger class every time `getInstance()` is called. To fix this, the constructor should be private, and the instance should be initialized only once.

2. Observer Pattern:
The Observer pattern facilitates a one-to-many dependency relationship between objects. It allows one object (the subject) to notify multiple other objects (observers) about any state changes, ensuring loose coupling between them. This pattern is useful when a change in one object requires updating several other related objects. For instance, in a weather application, multiple displays need to update their information whenever the weather data changes.

3. Factory Pattern:
The Factory pattern provides a way to create objects without exposing the instantiation logic to the client. It encapsulates the object creation process in a separate class, allowing flexibility in selecting the desired concrete class based on certain conditions. This pattern is useful when the client code needs to work with different implementations of an interface without being aware of their concrete classes. For example, consider a payment gateway system that provides different payment methods (e.g., credit card, PayPal, etc.). The Factory pattern can be employed to create the appropriate payment method object based on user preferences.

Erroneous code attempting to implement the Factory pattern:
```java
public interface PaymentMethod {
    void processPayment();
}

public class CreditCard implements PaymentMethod {
    public void processPayment() {
        // Credit card payment processing
    }
}

public class PayPal implements PaymentMethod {
    public void processPayment() {
        // PayPal payment processing
    }
}

public class PaymentFactory {
    public static PaymentMethod createPayment(String type) {
        if (type.equalsIgnoreCase("creditcard")) {
            return new CreditCard();
        } else if (type.equalsIgnoreCase("paypal")) {
            return new PayPal();
        }
        return null; // Missing return statement
    }
}
```
In this example, the code fails to adhere to the Factory pattern because it does not handle the case when an invalid type is passed to `createPayment()`. To fix this, a default behavior or an exception should be implemented when an unsupported type is encountered.

Overall, the OOP approach and its associated design patterns enhance code readability, maintainability, and flexibility, allowing developers to build robust and scalable software systems.

