Here's an implementation of the in-place insertion sort algorithm in Java that also removes duplicate elements from the sorted array:

```java
public class InsertionSort {

    public static int[] insertionSort(int[] array) {
        for (int i = 1; i < array.length; i++) {
            int key = array[i];
            int j = i - 1;

            while (j >= 0 && array[j] > key) {
                array[j + 1] = array[j];
                j--;
            }

            array[j + 1] = key;
        }

        int[] uniqueArray = new int[array.length];
        int uniqueIndex = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
            if (i == 0 || array[i] != array[i - 1]) {
                uniqueArray[uniqueIndex] = array[i];
                uniqueIndex++;
            }
        }

        int[] result = new int[uniqueIndex];
        System.arraycopy(uniqueArray, 0, result, 0, uniqueIndex);
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {5, 2, 8, 3, 5, 1, 2, 8, 5, 4};
        int[] sortedArray = insertionSort(array);

        System.out.println("Sorted Array with Duplicates Removed: ");
        for (int num : sortedArray) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

The insertionSort method implements the in-place insertion sort algorithm, where it iterates through the array and moves each element to its correct position in the sorted subarray.

After sorting the array, a separate uniqueArray is created to store the sorted and unique elements. A uniqueIndex variable keeps track of the current index in the uniqueArray. The elements are added to the uniqueArray only if they are different from the previous element.

Finally, the uniqueArray is copied to a result array of the correct size and returned as the output.

The main method demonstrates the usage of the insertionSort method by sorting an example array and printing the sorted array with duplicate elements removed.

