Here is a JavaScript code snippet that checks if a cookie named "username" exists in the browser, retrieves its content, and removes the cookie using Promises:

```javascript
// Function to check if a cookie exists
function checkCookieExists(cookieName) {
  return new Promise(function(resolve, reject) {
    // Retrieve all cookies
    var cookies = document.cookie.split(";");

    // Loop through the cookies
    for (var i = 0; i < cookies.length; i++) {
      var cookie = cookies[i].trim();

      // Check if the cookie name matches
      if (cookie.indexOf(cookieName + "=") === 0) {
        resolve(cookie.split("=")[1]); // Resolve with the cookie value
        return;
      }
    }

    reject("No " + cookieName + " cookie found"); // Reject if the cookie does not exist
  });
}

// Function to remove a cookie
function removeCookie(cookieName) {
  document.cookie = cookieName + "=; expires=Thu, 01 Jan 1970 00:00:00 UTC; path=/;";
}

// Check if "username" cookie exists
checkCookieExists("username")
  .then(function(cookieValue) {
    console.log("Username cookie found: " + cookieValue);
    removeCookie("username");
    console.log("Username cookie removed");
  })
  .catch(function(error) {
    console.log(error);
  });
```

This code uses the `checkCookieExists` function to check if the "username" cookie exists. If the cookie exists, it resolves with the cookie value. Otherwise, it rejects with an error message.

In the `then` callback, we print the content of the cookie to the console, remove the cookie using the `removeCookie` function, and print a success message.

In the `catch` callback, we display the error message if the cookie does not exist.

