Here's a Java program that generates the first 100 prime numbers and prints out their multiplication table in descending order:

```java
public class PrimeMultiplicationTable {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int numberOfPrimes = 100;
        int[] primes = generatePrimes(numberOfPrimes);
        printMultiplicationTable(primes);
    }

    public static int[] generatePrimes(int numberOfPrimes) {
        int[] primes = new int[numberOfPrimes];
        primes[0] = 2;
        int count = 1;
        int number = 3;
        while (count < numberOfPrimes) {
            boolean isPrime = true;
            for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
                if (number % primes[i] == 0) {
                    isPrime = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (isPrime) {
                primes[count] = number;
                count++;
            }
            number += 2; // Increment by 2 to skip even numbers
        }
        return primes;
    }

    public static void printMultiplicationTable(int[] primes) {
        int tableSize = primes.length;
        for (int i = tableSize - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            for (int j = tableSize - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
                System.out.printf("%4d", primes[i] * primes[j]);
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

The `generatePrimes` method uses a custom prime number generation algorithm to generate the first `numberOfPrimes` prime numbers. It starts with the first prime number 2 and then checks each odd number starting from 3 to see if it is divisible by any of the previously generated prime numbers. If it is not divisible, it is considered a prime number and added to the array of primes.

The `printMultiplicationTable` method takes the array of primes and prints out the multiplication table in descending order. It uses two nested loops to iterate through the primes array and multiplies each pair of primes. The `System.out.printf` method is used to format the output with a fixed width of 4 digits for each multiplication result.

Note: Generating the first 100 prime numbers using a custom algorithm can be time-consuming. You may need to wait for a few seconds for the program to generate the primes and print the multiplication table.

