```java
// Interface with a method
interface InterfaceA {
    void methodA();
}

// Abstract class with a method
abstract class AbstractB {
    abstract void methodB();
}

// Class that implements InterfaceA and extends AbstractB
class MyClass extends AbstractB implements InterfaceA {
    void methodC() {
        System.out.println("Method C");
    }

    // Method overriding from InterfaceA
    @Override
    public void methodA() {
        System.out.println("Method A");
    }

    // Method overriding from AbstractB
    @Override
    void methodB() {
        System.out.println("Method B");
    }

    // Method overloading
    void methodB(String str) {
        System.out.println("Method B with String: " + str);
    }
}

// Main class
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass obj = new MyClass();
        obj.methodA();
        obj.methodB();
        obj.methodB("Overloaded");
        obj.methodC();
    }
}
```

Output:
```
Method A
Method B
Method B with String: Overloaded
Method C
```

In this program, we have an interface `InterfaceA` with a single method `methodA()`, and an abstract class `AbstractB` with a single abstract method `methodB()`. The class `MyClass` implements `InterfaceA` and extends `AbstractB`, therefore inheriting both the interface and the abstract class.

In `MyClass`, we override the methods `methodA()` and `methodB()` from `InterfaceA` and `AbstractB` respectively, providing our own implementation for each method. We also demonstrate method overloading by creating an additional method `methodB(String str)` with a different parameter.

Finally, in the `Main` class, we create an object of `MyClass` and call the methods `methodA()`, `methodB()`, `methodB("Overloaded")`, and `methodC()` to demonstrate the functionality of the class hierarchy and the implementation of the interface and abstract class.

