Adding two matrices using only bitwise operations and without using any arithmetic operators in C++ can be achieved by manipulating the bits of the matrices.

Here's an example implementation:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <bitset>

using namespace std;

const int MATRIX_SIZE = 3;  // Change this to match the desired matrix size

// Function to add two matrices using bitwise operations
void addMatrices(int matrix1[MATRIX_SIZE][MATRIX_SIZE], int matrix2[MATRIX_SIZE][MATRIX_SIZE], int result[MATRIX_SIZE][MATRIX_SIZE]) {
    for (int i = 0; i < MATRIX_SIZE; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < MATRIX_SIZE; j++) {
            int carry = 0;
            int sum = 0;

            for (int k = 0; k < sizeof(int) * 8; k++) {
                int bit1 = (matrix1[i][j] >> k) & 1;
                int bit2 = (matrix2[i][j] >> k) & 1;

                // Bitwise XOR to calculate sum without carry
                int sumBit = bit1 ^ bit2 ^ carry;

                // Bitwise OR to calculate carry
                carry = (bit1 & bit2) | (carry & (bit1 ^ bit2));

                // Shift the sum bit to its position
                sum |= (sumBit << k);
            }

            // Assign the calculated sum to the result matrix
            result[i][j] = sum;
        }
    }
}

// Function to display a matrix
void displayMatrix(int matrix[MATRIX_SIZE][MATRIX_SIZE]) {
    for (int i = 0; i < MATRIX_SIZE; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < MATRIX_SIZE; j++) {
            cout << bitset<32>(matrix[i][j]) << " ";
        }
        cout << endl;
    }
}

int main() {
    int matrix1[MATRIX_SIZE][MATRIX_SIZE] = {
        {5, 9, 3},
        {2, 7, 1},
        {6, 4, 8}
    };

    int matrix2[MATRIX_SIZE][MATRIX_SIZE] = {
        {1, 6, 4},
        {8, 3, 2},
        {7, 9, 5}
    };

    int result[MATRIX_SIZE][MATRIX_SIZE] = {0};

    addMatrices(matrix1, matrix2, result);

    cout << "Matrix 1:" << endl;
    displayMatrix(matrix1);

    cout << endl << "Matrix 2:" << endl;
    displayMatrix(matrix2);

    cout << endl << "Resultant Matrix:" << endl;
    displayMatrix(result);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `addMatrices` function performs the addition operation on the matrices using bitwise operations. The function iterates over each element of the matrices, extracts each bit of the numbers, performs bitwise XOR and OR operations to calculate the sum and carry, and finally constructs the resulting sum using bitwise OR. The calculated sum is then assigned to the result matrix.

The `displayMatrix` function is used to display the matrices in binary format using `std::bitset`.

Please note that this approach is purely for demonstration purposes and may not be efficient or practical for larger matrices.

