Here is a C++ program that inserts an element into a given vector at a specific position and then sorts the vector in descending order:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}; // Initial vector

    int element, position;

    // Input element to insert
    std::cout << "Enter element to insert: ";
    std::cin >> element;

    // Input position to insert
    std::cout << "Enter position to insert: ";
    std::cin >> position;

    // Check if position is valid
    if (position < 0 || position > nums.size()) {
        std::cout << "Invalid position." << std::endl;
        return 0;
    }

    // Insert element at specified position
    nums.insert(nums.begin() + position, element);

    // Sort vector in descending order
    std::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end(), std::greater<int>());

    // Print the sorted vector
    std::cout << "Sorted Vector: ";
    for (int num : nums) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we first declare a vector called `nums` and initialize it with some values. We then prompt the user to enter the element to insert and the position at which to insert it. After that, we check if the position is valid (i.e., within the range of the vector). If it is valid, we use the `insert()` function to insert the element at the specified position. Finally, we use the `sort()` function from the `<algorithm>` library to sort the vector in descending order using the `std::greater<int>` comparator. The sorted vector is then printed to the console.

