Here is an implementation of the three-way quicksort algorithm in Java:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class ThreeWayQuickSort {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6, 3};
        System.out.println("Original array: " + Arrays.toString(arr));

        quicksort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);

        System.out.println("Sorted array: " + Arrays.toString(arr));
    }

    public static void quicksort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            int[] partitionIndices = partition(arr, low, high);
            quicksort(arr, low, partitionIndices[0] - 1);
            quicksort(arr, partitionIndices[1] + 1, high);
        }
    }

    public static int[] partition(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        int pivot = arr[low];
        int lt = low;
        int gt = high;
        int i = low + 1;

        while (i <= gt) {
            if (arr[i] < pivot) {
                swap(arr, lt, i);
                lt++;
                i++;
            } else if (arr[i] > pivot) {
                swap(arr, i, gt);
                gt--;
            } else {
                i++;
            }
        }
        return new int[] {lt, gt};
    }

    public static void swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
    }
}
```

In this implementation, the `quicksort` method recursively sorts the array by selecting a pivot element and partitioning the array into three parts - elements less than the pivot, elements equal to the pivot, and elements greater than the pivot. The `partition` method is responsible for rearranging the array based on the pivot element, and it returns an array of two indices - `lt` and `gt`, which represent the indices of the first and last element of the partition with elements equal to the pivot.

The `swap` method is a helper method that swaps two elements in the array.

The `main` method demonstrates the usage of the `quicksort` method on an example array.

