Here's an algorithm in Java that prints the prime numbers between 1 and 1000, without using any built-in functions or libraries to determine primality:

```java
public class PrimeNumbers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        printPrimeNumbers(1000);
    }

    public static void printPrimeNumbers(int limit) {
        // Start from 2, as 1 is not a prime number
        for (int number = 2; number <= limit; number++) {
            boolean isPrime = true;

            // Check divisibility by numbers from 2 to sqrt(number)
            for (int divisor = 2; divisor <= Math.sqrt(number); divisor++) {
                if (number % divisor == 0) {
                    isPrime = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (isPrime) {
                System.out.print(number + " ");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this algorithm, we start from 2 (as 1 is not a prime number) and iterate through each number up to the given limit (in this case, 1000). For each number, we check its divisibility by numbers from 2 to the square root of the number. If it is divisible by any number in that range, it is not a prime number and we break out of the loop. If it is not divisible by any number, it is a prime number and we print it.

By only checking divisibility up to the square root of the number, we optimize the algorithm by reducing the number of iterations required, as any factor of a number must be less than or equal to its square root.

Here's an implementation of a C++ program that meets all the requirements mentioned:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;

int exponentialInterpolationSearch(int arr[], int n, int target) {
    if (arr[0] > arr[n-1]) {
        cout << "Error: Array is not sorted in ascending order.";
        return -1;
    }

    int low = 0;
    int high = n - 1;
    int pos;

    while (low <= high && target >= arr[low] && target <= arr[high]) {
        if (low == high) {
            if (arr[low] == target)
                return low;
            return -1;
        }

        pos = low + ((double)(high - low) / (arr[high] - arr[low])) * (target - arr[low]);
        int value = arr[pos];

        if (value == target) {
            while (pos > 0 && arr[pos-1] == target)
                pos--;
            return pos;
        }

        if (value < target)
            low = pos + 1;
        else
            high = pos - 1;
    }

    return -1;
}

int main() {
    int n;
    cout << "Enter the size of the array: ";
    cin >> n;

    int arr[n];
    cout << "Enter the elements of the array in ascending order: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cin >> arr[i];
    }

    int target;
    cout << "Enter the target element to search: ";
    cin >> target;

    int index = exponentialInterpolationSearch(arr, n, target);

    if (index == -1)
        cout << "Target element not found in the array.";
    else
        cout << "Target element found at index: " << index;

    return 0;
}
```

