To create a doubly linked list in Java that satisfies the given constraints, follow these steps:

1. Create a Node class to represent each node of the doubly linked list. The Node class should have three properties: key, value, prev, and next.

```java
class Node {
    int key;
    Object value;
    Node prev;
    Node next;

    public Node(int key, Object value) {
        this.key = key;
        this.value = value;
        prev = null;
        next = null;
    }
}
```

2. Create a DoublyLinkedList class to handle the operations of the doubly linked list. The DoublyLinkedList class should have two properties: head and tail.

```java
class DoublyLinkedList {
    private Node head;
    private Node tail;

    // Constructor
    public DoublyLinkedList() {
        head = null;
        tail = null;
    }

    // Add a node at the head
    public void addAtHead(int key, Object value) {
        Node newNode = new Node(key, value);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
            tail = newNode;
        } else {
            newNode.next = head;
            head.prev = newNode;
            head = newNode;
        }
    }

    // Add a node at the tail
    public void addAtTail(int key, Object value) {
        Node newNode = new Node(key, value);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
            tail = newNode;
        } else {
            tail.next = newNode;
            newNode.prev = tail;
            tail = newNode;
        }
    }

    // Remove a node given its key
    public void remove(int key) throws Exception {
        if (head == null) {
            throw new Exception("List is empty");
        }

        Node currentNode = head;
        while (currentNode != null) {
            if (currentNode.key == key) {
                if (currentNode.prev != null) {
                    currentNode.prev.next = currentNode.next;
                } else {
                    head = currentNode.next;
                }

                if (currentNode.next != null) {
                    currentNode.next.prev = currentNode.prev;
                } else {
                    tail = currentNode.prev;
                }

                return;
            }

            currentNode = currentNode.next;
        }

        throw new Exception("Key not found in the list");
    }
}
```

Now, you can create an instance of the DoublyLinkedList class and perform the desired operations:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DoublyLinkedList list = new DoublyLinkedList();

        // Add nodes at the head
        list.addAtHead(1, "One");
        list.addAtHead(2, "Two");
        list.addAtHead(3, "Three");

        // Add nodes at the tail
        list.addAtTail(4, "Four");
        list.addAtTail(5, "Five");

        // Remove a node by key
        try {
            list.remove(3);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

This implementation allows you to create a doubly linked list with nodes containing both a key and a value, add nodes at the head or tail, and remove a node given its key while handling exceptions appropriately.

