Here is a Java program that prints all the prime numbers up to a given limit using the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class PrimeNumbersSieve {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int limit = 100; // define the limit

        boolean[] primes = sieveOfEratosthenes(limit);

        System.out.println("Prime numbers up to " + limit + ":");
        for (int i = 2; i <= limit; i++) {
            if (primes[i]) {
                System.out.print(i + " ");
            }
        }
    }

    private static boolean[] sieveOfEratosthenes(int limit) {
        boolean[] primes = new boolean[limit + 1];
        Arrays.fill(primes, true);

        primes[0] = primes[1] = false;

        for (int i = 2; i * i <= limit; i++) {
            if (primes[i]) {
                for (int j = i * i; j <= limit; j += i) {
                    primes[j] = false;
                }
            }
        }

        return primes;
    }
}
```

In this program, we first define the limit (in this case, 100). We then create a boolean array `primes` to track if a number is prime or not. We initialize all elements of this array as `true` since initially we assume that all numbers are prime.

Next, we iterate through the numbers starting from 2 up to the square root of the limit using the outer `for` loop. If the number is marked as prime (`primes[i]` is `true`), we mark all its multiples as non-prime by setting their corresponding array elements as `false`.

Finally, we print out all the numbers that are still marked as prime.

