        System.out.println("Final value of x: " + x);
    }

    private static void increment() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            x++;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

In this version, the `increment()` method acquires the lock before incrementing the value of `x`, and releases the lock in a `finally` block to ensure it is always released, even if an exception occurs. This guarantees that only one thread can increment `x` at a time, preventing race conditions.

