The time complexity of a binary search tree (BST) depends on the operation being performed. 

For searching, inserting, and deleting elements in a balanced BST, the time complexity is O(log n), where n is the number of nodes in the tree. This is because, in each step, the search or insertion operation reduces the search space by half, leading to a logarithmic time complexity.

However, if the BST is not balanced and its structure becomes skewed, the worst-case time complexity for searching, inserting, and deleting can become O(n), where n is the number of nodes. This can happen if elements are inserted in a sorted or nearly sorted order, resulting in an unbalanced tree with one-sided branches.

