To merge two linked lists in ascending order while maintaining the original order of elements within each linked list, we can use a modified version of the merge sort algorithm. Here's a Java program that implements this:

```java
import java.util.HashSet;

class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    public Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

public class MergeLinkedLists {
    public static Node merge(Node head1, Node head2) {
        // Create a dummy node to hold the merged list
        Node dummy = new Node(0);
        Node tail = dummy;

        // Traverse both lists and merge them in ascending order
        while (head1 != null && head2 != null) {
            if (head1.data < head2.data) {
                tail.next = head1;
                head1 = head1.next;
            } else if (head1.data > head2.data) {
                tail.next = head2;
                head2 = head2.next;
            } else {
                // Skip duplicates
                head1 = head1.next;
                head2 = head2.next;
            }
            tail = tail.next;
        }

        // Append the remaining elements of the first list
        while (head1 != null) {
            tail.next = head1;
            head1 = head1.next;
            tail = tail.next;
        }

        // Append the remaining elements of the second list
        while (head2 != null) {
            tail.next = head2;
            head2 = head2.next;
            tail = tail.next;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create the first linked list: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 6 -> 9
        Node head1 = new Node(1);
        head1.next = new Node(2);
        head1.next.next = new Node(3);
        head1.next.next.next = new Node(6);
        head1.next.next.next.next = new Node(9);

        // Create the second linked list: 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 7
        Node head2 = new Node(2);
        head2.next = new Node(3);
        head2.next.next = new Node(4);
        head2.next.next.next = new Node(7);

        // Merge the two linked lists
        Node mergedHead = merge(head1, head2);

        // Print the merged linked list
        Node current = mergedHead;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.print(current.data + " ");
            current = current.next;
        }
    }
}
```

Output:
```
1 2 3 4 6 7 9
```

In this program, we use a dummy node (`dummy`) to hold the merged list and a `tail` node to keep track of the last node in the merged list. We traverse both lists simultaneously and compare the values of the current nodes. We append the node with the smaller value to the merged list and move the corresponding head pointer forward. If the values are equal, we skip the duplicates. Finally, we append the remaining elements of the lists to the merged list.

The time complexity of this program is O(n), where n is the total number of elements in both linked lists, because we traverse each element exactly once. The space complexity is O(1) because we only use a constant amount of extra space to hold the dummy node and the tail node.

