function quicksort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) {
    return arr;
  }

  const pivot = arr[arr.length - 1];
  const left = [];
  const right = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < pivot) {
      left.push(arr[i]);
    } else {
      right.push(arr[i]);
    }
  }

  return [...quicksort(left), pivot, ...quicksort(right)];
}
```

To test the algorithm, you can call the `quicksort` function with an array of numbers:

```javascript
const unsortedArray = [8, 3, 5, 1, -2, 10, 4];
const sortedArray = quicksort(unsortedArray);

console.log(sortedArray);
// Output: [-2, 1, 3, 4, 5, 8, 10]
```

This implementation has a time complexity of O(n log n) because it divides the input array into two smaller arrays at each recursive call. The worst-case time complexity is O(n^2) if the chosen pivot is consistently the smallest or largest element in the array, but this can be mitigated by selecting a random pivot or using other pivot selection strategies.

