Here is a solution to check if a number is prime and includes the "isPrimeFast" method that implements the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool;
import java.util.concurrent.RecursiveTask;

public class PrimeChecker {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long number = 1000000000000L;

        // Check if the number is prime
        if (isPrime(number)) {
            System.out.println(number + " is a prime number.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(number + " is not a prime number.");
        }

        // Find prime numbers and calculate the sum
        long sum = findPrimesInRange(1, number);
        System.out.println("Sum of prime numbers in the range: " + sum);
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(long number) {
        if (number <= 1) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
            if (number % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    public static long findPrimesInRange(long start, long end) {
        PrimeTask task = new PrimeTask(start, end);
        ForkJoinPool pool = new ForkJoinPool();
        return pool.invoke(task);
    }

    public static class PrimeTask extends RecursiveTask<Long> {
        private static final long THRESHOLD = 1000;
        private long start;
        private long end;

        public PrimeTask(long start, long end) {
            this.start = start;
            this.end = end;
        }

        @Override
        protected Long compute() {
            if (end - start <= THRESHOLD) {
                return countPrimes(start, end);
            } else {
                long mid = (start + end) / 2;
                PrimeTask leftTask = new PrimeTask(start, mid);
                PrimeTask rightTask = new PrimeTask(mid + 1, end);

                leftTask.fork();
                long rightResult = rightTask.compute();
                long leftResult = leftTask.join();

                return leftResult + rightResult;
            }
        }

        private long countPrimes(long start, long end) {
            long count = 0;
            for (long i = start; i <= end; i++) {
                if (isPrimeFast(i)) {
                    count++;
                }
            }
            return count;
        }
    }

    public static boolean isPrimeFast(long number) {
        if (number <= 1) {
            return false;
        }

        // Create a boolean array "isPrime[0..number]" and initialize
        // all entries it as true. A value in isPrime[i] will finally
        // be false if i is Not a prime, else true.
        boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[(int) (number + 1)];
        for (int i = 0; i <= number; i++) {
            isPrime[i] = true;
        }

        for (int p = 2; p * p <= number; p++) {
            // If isPrime[p] is not changed, then it is a prime
            if (isPrime[p]) {
                // Update all multiples of p
                for (int i = p * p; i <= number; i += p) {
                    isPrime[i] = false;
                }
            }
        }

        return isPrime[(int) number];
    }
}
```

In the `isPrimeFast` method, the logical error is the condition for the outer loop. Currently, it checks `p * p <= number`, but it should be `p * p <= Math.sqrt(number)` to correctly iterate until the square root of the number. This error causes the algorithm to run longer than necessary. The fixed implementation of `isPrimeFast` would be:

```java
public static boolean isPrimeFast(long number) {
    if (number <= 1) {
        return false;
    }

    boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[(int) (number + 1)];
    for (int i = 0; i <= number; i++) {
        isPrime[i] = true;
    }

    for (int p = 2; p * p <= Math.sqrt(number); p++) {
        if (isPrime[p]) {
            for (int i = p * p; i <= number; i += p) {
                isPrime[i] = false;
            }
        }
    }

    return isPrime[(int) number];
}
