Here's a Java function that finds the length of the longest common subsequence of two strings and returns the actual subsequence:

```java
public class LongestCommonSubsequence {
    public static String findLCS(String str1, String str2) {
        int[][] dp = new int[str1.length() + 1][str2.length() + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= str1.length(); i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= str2.length(); j++) {
                if (str1.charAt(i - 1) == str2.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i][j - 1], dp[i - 1][j]);
                }
            }
        }

        int length = dp[str1.length()][str2.length()];
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        int i = str1.length(), j = str2.length();

        while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
            if (str1.charAt(i - 1) == str2.charAt(j - 1)) {
                sb.insert(0, str1.charAt(i - 1));
                i--;
                j--;
            } else if (dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]) {
                i--;
            } else {
                j--;
            }
        }

        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str1 = "Hello, world!";
        String str2 = "Helds frrld";

        String lcs = findLCS(str1, str2);
        int length = lcs.length();

        System.out.println("Length of LCS: " + length);
        System.out.println("LCS: " + lcs);
    }
}
```

In this implementation, we use dynamic programming to construct a 2D matrix `dp` to store the lengths of the longest common subsequences of substrings of `str1` and `str2`. The value `dp[i][j]` represents the length of the longest common subsequence of `str1.substring(0, i)` and `str2.substring(0, j)`.

We iterate through the characters of `str1` and `str2` using nested loops. If the characters at the current positions are equal, we increment the length by 1 and update `dp[i][j]` accordingly. Otherwise, we take the maximum length from either moving one step back in `str1` or one step back in `str2`.

After filling the `dp` matrix, we can reconstruct the longest common subsequence by backtracking from the bottom-right corner. Starting from the last characters of `str1` and `str2`, we check if they are equal. If they are, we add the character to the beginning of a `StringBuilder` and move diagonally up-left in the matrix. If they are not equal, we move either up or left, depending on which adjacent cell in the matrix has a higher length.

Finally, we return the longest common subsequence and its length. In the provided `main` method, we demonstrate how to use the `findLCS` function by finding the LCS of two example strings.

