Here is a JavaScript program that detects if a given element exists in an array or not, handling cases where the search element is not of the same type as the elements in the array:

```javascript
function searchElement(arr, searchElement) {
  // Sort the array in ascending order
  arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);

  let left = 0;
  let right = arr.length - 1;

  // Perform binary search
  while (left <= right) {
    let middle = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);

    // Convert search element to string for comparison
    let searchElementString = String(searchElement);

    // Convert middle element to string for comparison
    let middleElementString = String(arr[middle]);

    if (searchElementString === middleElementString) {
      return true;
    } else if (searchElementString < middleElementString) {
      right = middle - 1;
    } else {
      left = middle + 1;
    }
  }

  return false;
}

const arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1];
const searchElement = "3";

console.log(searchElement(arr, searchElement)); // Output: true
```

In this program, we first sort the array in ascending order using the `sort()` method. Then, we perform binary search by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half until the search element is found or the search interval becomes empty. 

Before comparing the search element with the middle element of the array, we convert both of them to strings using the `String()` function. This ensures that we can handle cases where the search element is not of the same type as the elements in the array.

The time complexity of this program is O(log n) because the binary search algorithm reduces the search interval by half in each iteration.

