Here is a Java method that returns the largest prime number less than a given number, with a time complexity of O(sqrt(n)):

```java
public class LargestPrimeNumber {

    public static int getLargestPrime(int number) {
        // Check if the number is less than 2
        if (number < 2) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Number must be greater than or equal to 2");
        }

        // Start checking for prime numbers from number-1, going backwards
        for (int i = number - 1; i >= 2; i--) {
            // Check if i is a prime number
            if (isPrime(i)) {
                return i; // Return the largest prime number found
            }
        }

        throw new IllegalArgumentException("No prime number found less than " + number);
    }

    private static boolean isPrime(int number) {
        // Check if the number is divisible by any number from 2 to its square root
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
            if (number % i == 0) {
                return false; // Number is not prime
            }
        }

        return true; // Number is prime
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 20;
        int largestPrime = getLargestPrime(number);
        System.out.println("The largest prime number less than " + number + " is: " + largestPrime);
    }
}
```

In the `getLargestPrime` method, we start checking for prime numbers from `number - 1` and go backwards. For each number, we call the `isPrime` method to check if it is prime. The `isPrime` method checks if the number is divisible by any number from 2 to its square root. If the number is divisible by any of these numbers, it is not prime. If the number is not divisible by any of these numbers, it is prime. Once we find a prime number, we return it as the largest prime number less than the given number.

The time complexity of this method is O(sqrt(n)), as the `isPrime` method checks divisibility up to the square root of the number.

