Here is a C++ program that prompts the user to enter an email address, validates whether it is a valid email address or not, and checks if the domain of the email address exists and is reachable.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>
#include <cstdlib>

bool validateEmail(const std::string& email) {
    // Regular expression pattern for email validation
    std::regex pattern(R"((\w+)(\.|_)?(\w*)@(\w+)(\.\w{2,3}){1,2})");

    return std::regex_match(email, pattern);
}

bool checkDomain(const std::string& domain) {
    // Command to check domain reachability using ping command
    std::string command = "ping -c 1 " + domain;

    // Execute the command and capture the output
    std::string result = "";
    FILE* pipe = popen(command.c_str(), "r");
    if (!pipe) {
        return false;
    }

    char buffer[128];
    while (!feof(pipe)) {
        if (fgets(buffer, 128, pipe) != nullptr) {
            result += buffer;
        }
    }

    pclose(pipe);

    // Check if the output contains "1 packets transmitted"
    return result.find("1 packets transmitted") != std::string::npos;
}

int main() {
    std::string email;
    std::cout << "Enter an email address: ";
    std::cin >> email;

    if (validateEmail(email)) {
        std::string domain = email.substr(email.find('@') + 1);

        if (checkDomain(domain)) {
            std::cout << "Congratulations! Your email address is valid." << std::endl;
        } else {
            std::cout << "Sorry, the domain of the email address you entered is not reachable." << std::endl;
        }
    } else {
        std::cout << "Sorry, the email address you entered is not valid." << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we use regular expressions to validate the email address format. The regular expression pattern `(\w+)(\.|_)?(\w*)@(\w+)(\.\w{2,3}){1,2}` matches the common email address format.

The `validateEmail()` function checks if the email matches the regular expression pattern and returns a boolean value.

The `checkDomain()` function uses the `ping` command to check if the domain is reachable. It executes the command by opening a pipe and reading the output of the command. If the output contains the string "1 packets transmitted", it means the domain is reachable, and the function returns `true`.

In the `main()` function, we prompt the user to enter an email address and validate it using the `validateEmail()` function. If the email address is valid, we extract the domain and check its reachability using the `checkDomain()` function. Finally, we display the appropriate message based on the validation results.

