Here's an example implementation of an Angular directive that meets the given requirements:

```javascript
angular.module('myApp').directive('myRequiredInput', function() {
  return {
    restrict: 'A',
    require: 'ngModel',
    scope: {
      myPattern: '=',
      myErrorMessage: '@'
    },
    link: function(scope, element, attrs, ngModelCtrl) {
      // Add a red border to the text field when it is empty or does not match the specified format
      var addErrorStyle = function() {
        element.css('border', '1px solid red');
      };

      // Remove the red border when the input is not empty and matches the specified format
      var removeErrorStyle = function() {
        element.css('border', '');
      };

      // Display an error message below the text field when it is empty or does not match the specified format
      var displayErrorMessage = function() {
        var errorElement = angular.element('<div class="error-message">' + scope.myErrorMessage + '</div>');
        element.after(errorElement);
      };

      // Remove the error message when the input is not empty and matches the specified format
      var removeErrorMessage = function() {
        element.next('.error-message').remove();
      };

      // Validate the input against the specified format
      var validateInput = function(inputValue) {
        if (!inputValue || !scope.myPattern.test(inputValue)) {
          addErrorStyle();
          displayErrorMessage();
          return false;
        } else {
          removeErrorStyle();
          removeErrorMessage();
          return true;
        }
      };

      // Watch for changes in the input value
      scope.$watch(function() {
        return ngModelCtrl.$modelValue;
      }, function(newValue) {
        validateInput(newValue);
      });

      // Watch for changes in the specified format
      scope.$watch('myPattern', function() {
        validateInput(ngModelCtrl.$modelValue);
      });

      // Watch for changes in the error message
      scope.$watch('myErrorMessage', function() {
        removeErrorMessage();
        validateInput(ngModelCtrl.$modelValue);
      });
    }
  };
});
```

In this example, the directive is named "myRequiredInput". It requires the "ngModel" directive and creates an isolated scope to receive the pattern and error message attributes.

The link function defines four helper functions: addErrorStyle, removeErrorStyle, displayErrorMessage, and removeErrorMessage. These functions are responsible for manipulating the DOM to apply or remove the required error styles and error message.

The validateInput function checks whether the input value is empty or matches the specified format. If it is invalid, it adds the error styles and error message. If it is valid, it removes the error styles and error message.

The $watch functions are used to watch for changes in the input value, the specified format, and the error message. Whenever these values change, the validateInput function is called to re-validate the input.

To use this directive, add the "my-required-input" attribute to the input element. You can also specify the pattern and error message using the "my-pattern" and "my-error-message" attributes, respectively.

