Here is the C++ function that calculates the sum of all prime factors of a given positive integer:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>

bool isPrime(int num) {
    if (num <= 1) {
        return false;
    }

    for (int i = 2; i <= sqrt(num); i++) {
        if (num % i == 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    return true;
}

int sumPrimeFactors(int num) {
    int sum = 0;

    for (int i = 2; i <= sqrt(num); i++) {
        if (num % i == 0 && isPrime(i)) {
            sum += i;
        }

        if (num % i == 0 && isPrime(num / i) && i != num / i) {
            sum += (num / i);
        }
    }

    return sum;
}

int main() {
    int num = 54;
    int sum = sumPrimeFactors(num);

    std::cout << "Sum of prime factors of " << num << " is " << sum << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

The function `isPrime()` is used to check if a number is prime or not. It iterates from 2 to the square root of the number and checks if the number is divisible by any number in that range. If it is divisible by any number, it is not prime and the function returns false. Otherwise, it is prime and the function returns true.

The function `sumPrimeFactors()` takes in a positive integer as input and iterates from 2 to the square root of the number. It checks if the number is divisible by the current iteration value and if the current iteration value is prime. If both conditions are true, the current iteration value is added to the sum. Additionally, if the number is divisible by the current iteration value and the quotient is prime and not equal to the current iteration value, the quotient is also added to the sum. Finally, the function returns the sum of the prime factors.

