Here is the code to check if a number is prime and the reference implementation of the "isPrimeFast" method with a mistake:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class PrimeNumberChecker {

    public static boolean isPrime(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
            if (num % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    public static boolean isPrimeFast(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) {
            return false;
        }

        boolean[] isComposite = new boolean[num + 1];

        for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
            if (!isComposite[i]) {
                for (int j = i * i; j <= num; j += i) {
                    isComposite[j] = true;
                }
            }
        }

        return !isComposite[num];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 1000000000;
        List<Integer> primeNumbers = new ArrayList<>();

        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

        for (int i = 2; i <= number; i++) {
            int finalI = i;
            executor.execute(() -> {
                if (isPrimeFast(finalI)) {
                    synchronized (primeNumbers) {
                        primeNumbers.add(finalI);
                    }
                }
            });
        }

        executor.shutdown();
        while (!executor.isTerminated()) {
            // Waiting for all threads to finish
        }

        int sum = 0;
        for (int prime : primeNumbers) {
            System.out.print(prime + ", ");
            sum += prime;
        }

        System.out.println("\nSum: " + sum);
    }
}
```

In the code above, we have implemented two methods: "isPrime" and "isPrimeFast". The "isPrime" method checks if a number is prime by iterating from 2 to the square root of the number and checking for divisibility. The "isPrimeFast" method uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to efficiently determine if a number is prime.

The main method generates a list of prime numbers in the range of 1 to the given number using multithreading. Each number is checked for primality using the "isPrimeFast" method, and if it is prime, it is added to the list of prime numbers. Finally, the program outputs the list of prime numbers and calculates their sum.

