Here is a JavaScript function that removes the last character of a string if it is a vowel, and also removes the second to last character if it is a consonant:

```javascript
function removeCharacters(str) {
  const vowels = ['a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'];
  const consonants = ['b', 'c', 'd', 'f', 'g', 'h', 'j', 'k', 'l', 'm', 'n', 'p', 'q', 'r', 's', 't', 'v', 'w', 'x', 'y', 'z'];

  if (vowels.includes(str[str.length - 1])) {
    str = str.slice(0, str.length - 1);
    if (consonants.includes(str[str.length - 1])) {
      str = str.slice(0, str.length - 1);
    }
  }

  return str;
}
```

