import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class PrimeFibonacciNumber {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter the value of n: ");
        int n = scanner.nextInt();

        if (n <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. n must be a positive integer.");
            return;
        }

        BigInteger primeFibonacci = getNthPrimeFibonacci(n);
        System.out.println("The " + n + "th prime Fibonacci number is: " + primeFibonacci);

        BigInteger sumOfPrimeFibonacci = getSumOfPrimeFibonacci(n);
        System.out.println("The sum of the first " + n + " prime Fibonacci numbers is: " + sumOfPrimeFibonacci);
    }

    public static BigInteger getNthPrimeFibonacci(int n) {
        BigInteger prev = BigInteger.ONE;
        BigInteger curr = BigInteger.ONE;
        int count = 2;

        while (count < n) {
            BigInteger next = prev.add(curr);

            if (isPrime(next)) {
                count++;
            }

            prev = curr;
            curr = next;
        }

        return curr;
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(BigInteger num) {
        if (num.compareTo(BigInteger.ONE) <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        for (BigInteger i = BigInteger.TWO; i.multiply(i).compareTo(num) <= 0; i = i.add(BigInteger.ONE)) {
            if (num.mod(i).equals(BigInteger.ZERO)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    public static BigInteger getSumOfPrimeFibonacci(int n) {
        BigInteger sum = BigInteger.ZERO;
        BigInteger prev = BigInteger.ONE;
        BigInteger curr = BigInteger.ONE;
        int count = 2;

        while (count <= n) {
            BigInteger next = prev.add(curr);

            if (isPrime(next)) {
                sum = sum.add(next);
                count++;
            }

            prev = curr;
            curr = next;
        }

        return sum;
    }
}
```

In this script, we define a `getNthPrimeFibonacci` method that takes an integer `n` as input and returns the `n`th prime Fibonacci number using an optimized algorithm. The method iteratively generates Fibonacci numbers until it finds the `n`th prime Fibonacci number.

The `isPrime` method is used to check if a given number is prime. It iterates from 2 to the square root of the number and checks if any of the numbers divide the given number. If a divisor is found, the number is not prime. Otherwise, it is prime.

The `getSumOfPrimeFibonacci` method is used to calculate the sum of the first `n` prime Fibonacci numbers. It follows a similar approach as the `getNthPrimeFibonacci` method, but keeps track of the sum along the way.

The `main` method of the script prompts the user to enter the value of `n`, calls the `getNthPrimeFibonacci` and `getSumOfPrimeFibonacci` methods, and displays the results.

To handle large values of `n`, the script uses the `BigInteger` class to represent large integers. This allows for accurate calculations and handling of very large prime Fibonacci numbers.

The script also provides error handling for invalid inputs, such as negative values or non-integer values. If an invalid input is provided, an error message is displayed and the program terminates.

To find the sum of the first `n` prime Fibonacci numbers, the script calls the `getSumOfPrimeFibonacci` method and displays the result.

To optimize the script further for larger values of `n`, you can implement memoization to store previously calculated prime Fibonacci numbers and reuse them instead of recalculating. This can significantly reduce the time complexity for larger values of `n`.

