To optimize the time complexity of calculating the average temperature to O(1), we can create a new class that extends the original class and keeps track of the total temperature and count of readings for each month and year. 

Here's an example implementation:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class TemperatureReading {
    private int day;
    private int month;
    private int year;
    private String location;
    private double temperature;

    public TemperatureReading(int day, int month, int year, String location, double temperature) {
        this.day = day;
        this.month = month;
        this.year = year;
        this.location = location;
        this.temperature = temperature;
    }

    public static class MonthlyTemperature {
        private Map<Integer, Map<Integer, Double>> monthlyTemperatures;
        private Map<Integer, Map<Integer, Integer>> monthlyCounts;

        public MonthlyTemperature() {
            monthlyTemperatures = new HashMap<>();
            monthlyCounts = new HashMap<>();
        }

        public void addTemperature(TemperatureReading reading) {
            int month = reading.month;
            int year = reading.year;
            double temperature = reading.temperature;

            monthlyTemperatures.putIfAbsent(year, new HashMap<>());
            monthlyCounts.putIfAbsent(year, new HashMap<>());

            Map<Integer, Double> yearTemperatures = monthlyTemperatures.get(year);
            Map<Integer, Integer> yearCounts = monthlyCounts.get(year);

            yearTemperatures.put(month, yearTemperatures.getOrDefault(month, 0.0) + temperature);
            yearCounts.put(month, yearCounts.getOrDefault(month, 0) + 1);
        }

        public double getAverageTemperature(int month, int year) {
            Map<Integer, Double> yearTemperatures = monthlyTemperatures.get(year);
            Map<Integer, Integer> yearCounts = monthlyCounts.get(year);

            if (yearTemperatures != null && yearCounts != null) {
                double totalTemperature = yearTemperatures.getOrDefault(month, 0.0);
                int count = yearCounts.getOrDefault(month, 0);
                if (count > 0) {
                    return totalTemperature / count;
                }
            }
            return 0.0;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MonthlyTemperature monthlyTemperature = new MonthlyTemperature();

        // Assuming we have a list of temperature readings
        // Add temperature readings to monthlyTemperature
        monthlyTemperature.addTemperature(new TemperatureReading(1, 1, 2021, "Location1", 10.0));
        monthlyTemperature.addTemperature(new TemperatureReading(2, 1, 2021, "Location2", 15.0));
        monthlyTemperature.addTemperature(new TemperatureReading(3, 2, 2021, "Location1", 12.0));

        // Get average temperature for a specific month and year
        double averageTemperature = monthlyTemperature.getAverageTemperature(1, 2021);
        System.out.println("Average temperature for January 2021: " + averageTemperature);
    }
}
```

In this implementation, we create a new class `MonthlyTemperature` which keeps track of the total temperature and count of readings for each month and year. The `monthlyTemperatures` map stores the total temperature for each month and year, while the `monthlyCounts` map stores the count of readings for each month and year.

The `addTemperature` method adds a new temperature reading to the `monthlyTemperatures` and `monthlyCounts` maps.

The `getAverageTemperature` method takes a month and year as parameters and retrieves the total temperature and count from the maps. It calculates the average temperature by dividing the total temperature by the count. If the count is 0 or if the month and year are not found in the maps, it returns 0.0.

In the `main` method, we create an instance of `MonthlyTemperature` and add temperature readings to it. Then, we call the `getAverageTemperature` method to retrieve the average temperature for a specific month and year.

