To compute the Fibonacci sequence modulo a given prime number p, we can use the matrix exponentiation method. The algorithm will have a time complexity of O(log n), which is considered constant time for very large input values.

Here's the algorithm in Java:

```java
import java.math.BigInteger;

public class FibonacciModulo {
    public static BigInteger fibonacciModulo(BigInteger n, BigInteger p) {
        // Handle negative input values
        if (n.compareTo(BigInteger.ZERO) < 0) {
            // Change sign for negative n
            n = n.negate();

            // If n is even, then change the sign for p
            if (n.mod(BigInteger.valueOf(2)).equals(BigInteger.ZERO)) {
                p = p.negate();
            }
        }

        // Base cases
        if (n.equals(BigInteger.ZERO)) {
            return BigInteger.ZERO;
        }
        if (n.equals(BigInteger.ONE) || n.equals(BigInteger.valueOf(-1))) {
            return BigInteger.ONE;
        }

        // Create the Fibonacci matrix
        BigInteger[][] fibonacciMatrix = {{BigInteger.ONE, BigInteger.ONE}, {BigInteger.ONE, BigInteger.ZERO}};

        // Raise the matrix to the power of (n - 1)
        BigInteger[][] result = matrixPower(fibonacciMatrix, n.subtract(BigInteger.ONE), p);

        // Return the element at the first row and first column of the result matrix
        return result[0][0];
    }

    private static BigInteger[][] matrixPower(BigInteger[][] matrix, BigInteger exponent, BigInteger p) {
        BigInteger[][] result = {{BigInteger.ONE, BigInteger.ZERO}, {BigInteger.ZERO, BigInteger.ONE}};

        while (exponent.compareTo(BigInteger.ZERO) > 0) {
            if (exponent.mod(BigInteger.valueOf(2)).equals(BigInteger.ONE)) {
                result = matrixMultiply(result, matrix, p);
            }
            matrix = matrixMultiply(matrix, matrix, p);
            exponent = exponent.divide(BigInteger.valueOf(2));
        }

        return result;
    }

    private static BigInteger[][] matrixMultiply(BigInteger[][] matrix1, BigInteger[][] matrix2, BigInteger p) {
        BigInteger[][] result = new BigInteger[2][2];
        BigInteger a = matrix1[0][0].multiply(matrix2[0][0]).add(matrix1[0][1].multiply(matrix2[1][0]));
        BigInteger b = matrix1[0][0].multiply(matrix2[0][1]).add(matrix1[0][1].multiply(matrix2[1][1]));
        BigInteger c = matrix1[1][0].multiply(matrix2[0][0]).add(matrix1[1][1].multiply(matrix2[1][0]));
        BigInteger d = matrix1[1][0].multiply(matrix2[0][1]).add(matrix1[1][1].multiply(matrix2[1][1]));

        result[0][0] = a.mod(p);
        result[0][1] = b.mod(p);
        result[1][0] = c.mod(p);
        result[1][1] = d.mod(p);

        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigInteger n = new BigInteger("1000000000000000000");
        BigInteger p = new BigInteger("1000000007");

        BigInteger fibonacci = fibonacciModulo(n, p);
        System.out.println("Fibonacci(" + n + ") mod " + p + " = " + fibonacci);
    }
}
```

This algorithm computes the Fibonacci sequence modulo a given prime number p using matrix exponentiation. The matrix is raised to the power of (n - 1), where n is the input value. The result is the element at the first row and first column of the resulting matrix.

The algorithm also handles negative input values for n by changing the sign of n and p accordingly. If n is even, the sign of p is also changed.

To handle composite numbers p, you can modify the code to find the modular inverse of the element at the first row and first column of the resulting matrix, using the Chinese Remainder Theorem. However, this would increase the time complexity beyond O(1) and would require additional code modifications.

