Here is the corrected program to print the given matrix while ensuring that the sum of each row and each column is divisible by 3:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int arr[3][3] = {{1, 2, 3},
                     {4, 5, 6},
                     {7, 8, 9}};
    int rowSum[3] = {0};
    int colSum[3] = {0};

    // Calculate the sum of each row and each column
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
            rowSum[i] += arr[i][j];
            colSum[j] += arr[i][j];
        }
    }

    // Find the correction needed for each element
    int correction[3][3] = {0};
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
            correction[i][j] = ((rowSum[i] % 3) + (colSum[j] % 3)) % 3;
        }
    }

    // Print the corrected matrix
    printf("Corrected matrix:\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
            printf("%d ", arr[i][j] + correction[i][j]);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The bug in the initial code was that the array `arr` was not initialized correctly. The correct initialization is done using nested curly braces, as shown in the corrected code. Additionally, the bug in the correction calculation was that the individual row and column sums were not taken modulo 3 before adding them together. This caused incorrect correction values to be calculated. The corrected code now calculates the correction needed for each element based on the modulo 3 of the row and column sums.

