```javascript
function validateForm() {
  var nameInput = document.getElementById("name");
  var emailInput = document.getElementById("email");
  var phoneInput = document.getElementById("phone");

  var nameError = document.getElementById("nameError");
  var emailError = document.getElementById("emailError");
  var phoneError = document.getElementById("phoneError");

  var valid = true;

  // Validate name field
  if (nameInput.value === "" || !/^[a-zA-Z]+$/.test(nameInput.value)) {
    nameError.textContent = "Please enter a valid name";
    valid = false;
  } else {
    nameError.textContent = "";
  }

  // Validate email field
  if (emailInput.value === "" || !/^[a-zA-Z]+$/.test(emailInput.value)) {
    emailError.textContent = "Please enter a valid email";
    valid = false;
  } else {
    emailError.textContent = "";
  }

  // Validate phone field
  if (phoneInput.value === "" || !/^[a-zA-Z]+$/.test(phoneInput.value)) {
    phoneError.textContent = "Please enter a valid phone number";
    valid = false;
  } else {
    phoneError.textContent = "";
  }

  return valid;
}
```

In the above code, we first retrieve the input fields and error spans using their respective IDs. Then, when the form is submitted, the `validateForm` function is called.

Inside the function, we validate each input field by checking if it is empty or if it contains any non-alphabetic characters using a regular expression `/^[a-zA-Z]+$/`. If the validation fails, we display an error message next to the field and set the `valid` variable to `false`. If the validation passes, we clear the error message.

Finally, we return the `valid` variable. If it is `true`, the form is submitted, otherwise, it is prevented from being submitted.

