Here is an example of a GraphQL query that fetches the total number of post titles in a blog, including the number of comments for each post, and provides filtering and sorting options:

```graphql
query GetPosts($minComments: Int, $sortBy: String) {
  posts(minComments: $minComments, sortBy: $sortBy) {
    totalCount
    edges {
      node {
        title
        commentsCount
      }
    }
  }
}
```

In this query, we define two variables:

- `$minComments`: An optional variable to filter the posts based on the minimum number of comments.
- `$sortBy`: An optional variable to sort the posts based on the number of comments. It can have two possible values: "asc" for ascending order or "desc" for descending order.

To implement the server-side logic, you will need to create a resolver function that handles this query. Here is an example resolver function in JavaScript:

```javascript
const getPosts = (args) => {
  // Retrieve all posts from the database
  const posts = db.getPosts();

  // Filter the posts based on the minimum number of comments
  if (args.minComments) {
    posts = posts.filter((post) => post.commentsCount >= args.minComments);
  }

  // Sort the posts based on the number of comments
  if (args.sortBy) {
    posts = posts.sort((a, b) => {
      if (args.sortBy === 'asc') {
        return a.commentsCount - b.commentsCount;
      } else if (args.sortBy === 'desc') {
        return b.commentsCount - a.commentsCount;
      }
    });
  }

  // Return the total count and the posts
  return {
    totalCount: posts.length,
    edges: posts.map((post) => ({
      node: {
        title: post.title,
        commentsCount: post.commentsCount,
      },
    })),
  };
};
```

In this example, `db.getPosts()` represents a function that retrieves the posts from the database. You will need to replace it with your own implementation based on your data source.

Make sure to connect the resolver function to the `posts` field in your schema definition so that it is called when this query is executed.

