```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class PrimeNumberValidator {

    private static ArrayList<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();

    public static boolean isPrime(int n) throws Exception {
        if (n < 1 || n > 1000) {
            throw new Exception("Input integer must be within the range of 1 to 1000.");
        }
        if (n == 1) {
            return false;
        }
        if (n == 2 || n == 3) {
            return true;
        }
        if (n % 2 == 0 || n % 3 == 0) {
            return false;
        }
        int sqrt = (int) Math.sqrt(n) + 1;
        for (int i = 6; i <= sqrt; i += 6) {
            if (n % (i - 1) == 0 || n % (i + 1) == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static int getNextPrime(int n) throws Exception {
        if (n < 1 || n > 1000) {
            throw new Exception("Input integer must be within the range of 1 to 1000.");
        }
        if (primes.isEmpty()) {
            primes.add(2);
            primes.add(3);
        }
        int nextPrime = n + 1;
        while (true) {
            if (isPrime(nextPrime)) {
                primes.add(nextPrime);
                return nextPrime;
            }
            nextPrime++;
            if (nextPrime > 1000) {
                return -1;
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(isPrime(17)); // Output: true
            System.out.println(isPrime(18)); // Output: false
            System.out.println(getNextPrime(17)); // Output: 19
            System.out.println(getNextPrime(20)); // Output: 23
            System.out.println(getNextPrime(1000)); // Output: -1
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

This implementation uses the following approach to determine if a number is prime:
1. Check if the number is less than 1 or greater than 1000, and throw an exception if it is.
2. Check if the number is 1, in which case it is not prime.
3. Check if the number is 2 or 3, in which case it is prime.
4. Check if the number is divisible by 2 or 3, in which case it is not prime.
5. Iterate from 6 up to the square root of the number, checking if the number is divisible by `i - 1` or `i + 1`, where `i` is a multiple of 6. If it is divisible, then it is not prime.
6. If none of the above conditions are met, the number is prime.

The `getNextPrime` function uses the `isPrime` function to find the next prime number greater than the input number. It also stores previously calculated prime numbers in the `primes` ArrayList to optimize the process. If there is no prime number greater than the input number, it returns -1.

