To compute the GCD (Greatest Common Divisor) of two numbers without using any built-in functions, loops, or recursion, and with a time complexity of O(log(min(a, b))), we can use the Euclidean algorithm.

The Euclidean algorithm states that the GCD of two numbers a and b is equal to the GCD of b and the remainder of a divided by b. We can repeat this process until the remainder becomes 0, at which point the GCD will be the non-zero value of b.

Here is the code to compute the GCD using the Euclidean algorithm:

```
def gcd(a, b):
    # Base case: if b is 0, the GCD is a
    if b == 0:
        return abs(a)

    # Recursive case: compute the GCD of b and the remainder of a divided by b
    return gcd(b, a % b)
```

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log(min(a, b))) because the algorithm repeatedly reduces the size of the input by dividing a by b, and the remainder gets smaller with each iteration. The number of iterations required is proportional to the number of digits in the smaller number, which is logarithmic in the size of the number itself.

