To convert a LinkedList of Person objects to an array of objects while ensuring the array is sorted in ascending order based on the age attribute of the Person objects, we can follow these steps:

1. Create a List<Person> to store the distinct Person objects based on their age attribute.
2. Iterate through the LinkedList and add each Person object to the List<Person>.
3. Sort the List<Person> in ascending order based on the age attribute of the Person objects. This can be achieved by implementing a custom Comparator for the Person class.
4. Convert the List<Person> to an array of objects using the toArray() method.

Here's the Java code to implement the solution:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class LinkedListToArray {
    public static class Person {
        private int age;
        // Other attributes and methods

        public Person(int age) {
            this.age = age;
        }

        public int getAge() {
            return age;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList<Person> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();
        linkedList.add(new Person(30));
        linkedList.add(new Person(25));
        linkedList.add(new Person(40));
        linkedList.add(new Person(25));
        linkedList.add(new Person(35));

        // Step 1: Create a List<Person>
        List<Person> personList = new ArrayList<>();

        // Step 2: Add each Person object to the List<Person>
        for (Person person : linkedList) {
            personList.add(person);
        }

        // Step 3: Sort the List<Person> in ascending order based on age attribute
        personList.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(Person::getAge));

        // Step 4: Convert the List<Person> to an array of objects
        Object[] personArray = personList.toArray();

        // Print the array
        for (Object obj : personArray) {
            Person person = (Person) obj;
            System.out.println(person.getAge());
        }
    }
}
```

The output of this code will be:
```
25
30
35
40
```

This solution has a time complexity of O(nlogn) due to the sorting operation, and a space complexity of O(n) as we are storing the distinct Person objects in a separate List<Person>.

