Here's a Java program that finds the first non-repeated character in a string, while ignoring any whitespace characters:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "hello world";
        char result = findFirstNonRepeatedChar(str);
        System.out.println("The first non-repeated character is: " + result);
    }

    public static char findFirstNonRepeatedChar(String str) {
        // Remove whitespace characters from the string
        str = str.replaceAll("\\s", "");

        // Create a frequency array to keep track of character occurrences
        int[] frequency = new int[256];

        // Calculate the frequency of each character
        for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
            frequency[str.charAt(i)]++;
        }

        // Find the first non-repeated character
        for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
            if (frequency[str.charAt(i)] == 1) {
                return str.charAt(i);
            }
        }

        // If no non-repeated character is found, return a default value
        return '\0';
    }
}
```

The program first removes all whitespace characters from the given string using the `replaceAll()` method and a regular expression (`\\s` matches any whitespace character).

Then, it creates a frequency array of size 256 to keep track of character occurrences. The ASCII value of each character is used as an index in the frequency array, and the corresponding value represents the frequency of that character in the string.

After calculating the frequency of each character, the program loops through the string again and finds the first character with a frequency of 1, indicating it is non-repeated. This character is returned as the result.

If no non-repeated character is found, the program returns a default value (`'\0'`).

