Here is a possible implementation of a method that reverses a string in Java without using any built-in string reversal functions or additional data structures:

```java
public static String reverseString(String str) {
    char[] characters = str.toCharArray();
    int length = characters.length;

    for (int i = 0; i < length / 2; i++) {
        char temp = characters[i];
        characters[i] = characters[length - i - 1];
        characters[length - i - 1] = temp;
    }

    return new String(characters);
}
```

Explanation:

1. Convert the input string into a character array using the `toCharArray()` method.
2. Initialize a variable `length` to hold the length of the character array.
3. Use a loop to iterate from the beginning to the middle of the character array (i.e., `length / 2`).
4. Inside the loop, swap the characters at the current position `i` and its corresponding position from the end of the array (`length - i - 1`).
5. Finally, create a new string from the reversed character array using the `String` constructor.
6. Return the reversed string.

The time complexity of this method is O(n), where n is the length of the string. This is because the method iterates only through half of the string to perform the character swaps.

