Here's an example of how you can implement a sorting algorithm in Java to sort an array of objects based on a specific attribute:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Person implements Comparable<Person> {
    private String name;
    private int id;

    public Person(String name, int id) {
        this.name = name;
        this.id = id;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public int getId() {
        return id;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Person other) {
        int nameComparison = this.name.compareTo(other.name);
        if (nameComparison != 0) {
            return nameComparison;
        } else {
            return Integer.compare(this.id, other.id);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Person [name=" + name + ", id=" + id + "]";
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Person[] persons = {
                new Person("John", 2),
                new Person("Alice", 3),
                new Person("Bob", 1),
                new Person("Alice", 4),
                new Person("John", 1)
        };

        // Sort the array
        bubbleSort(persons);

        // Print the sorted array
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(persons));
    }

    private static void bubbleSort(Person[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (arr[j].compareTo(arr[j + 1]) > 0) {
                    // Swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
                    Person temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j + 1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a `Person` class that represents an object with a name and an ID. The `Person` class implements the `Comparable` interface to define the natural ordering of objects based on their names and IDs.

The `bubbleSort` method is a simple sorting algorithm that iteratively compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. This algorithm continues until the array is sorted.

In the `main` method, we create an array of `Person` objects and call the `bubbleSort` method to sort the array. Finally, we print the sorted array to verify the result.

Here is a Java code that creates a set from the given array using a divide and conquer algorithm:

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class SetFromArray {

    public static Set<Integer> createSet(int[] arr) {
        return createSet(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    private static Set<Integer> createSet(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
        Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

        if (start == end) {
            set.add(arr[start]);
        } else {
            int mid = (start + end) / 2;
            Set<Integer> leftSet = createSet(arr, start, mid);
            Set<Integer> rightSet = createSet(arr, mid + 1, end);
            mergeSets(leftSet, rightSet, set);
        }

        return set;
    }

    private static void mergeSets(Set<Integer> set1, Set<Integer> set2, Set<Integer> result) {
        for (int num : set1) {
            result.add(num);
        }

        for (int num : set2) {
            result.add(num);
