Function overloading in C++ refers to the ability to define multiple functions with the same name but different parameter types or number of parameters. This allows the programmer to use the same function name for similar operations, increasing code readability and reusability.

Example 1: Calculator class

```cpp
#include <iostream>

class Calculator {
public:
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
};

int main() {
    Calculator calc;

    int sum_int = calc.add(5, 10);
    std::cout << "Sum of integers: " << sum_int << std::endl;

    double sum_double = calc.add(3.14, 2.71);
    std::cout << "Sum of doubles: " << sum_double << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the Calculator class has two member functions named "add" with the same name but different parameter types. The first "add" function takes two integers as parameters and returns their sum, while the second "add" function takes two doubles as parameters and returns their sum. This allows the user to call the appropriate function based on the data types they are working with.

