Here's a program that removes duplicate characters from a string and returns the remaining characters in the order they appear in the original string:

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class RemoveDuplicates {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "aabbccddee";

        String result = removeDuplicates(str);
        System.out.println(result); // Output: abcde
    }

    public static String removeDuplicates(String str) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        Set<Character> set = new HashSet<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
            char c = str.charAt(i);

            // Ignore non-alphabetic characters
            if (!Character.isAlphabetic(c)) {
                continue;
            }

            // Add character to set if it's not already present
            if (set.add(Character.toLowerCase(c))) {
                sb.append(c);
            }
        }

        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

In this program, we iterate over each character in the input string. We use a `Set` to keep track of the characters we've already encountered. If a character is alphabetic (A-Z or a-z), we convert it to lowercase and check if it's already in the set. If it's not, we add it to both the set and a `StringBuilder` object. Finally, we return the resulting string after all characters have been processed.

Here's an example of a JavaScript function that capitalizes the first letter of each word in a sentence while ignoring words that contain numbers or special characters:

```javascript
function capitalizeWords(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(' ');

  // Loop through each word
  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    const word = words[i];

    // Check if the word contains numbers or special characters
    if (/[\d\W]/.test(word)) {
      continue; // Ignore the word
    }

    // Capitalize the first letter of the word
    const capitalizedWord = word.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + word.slice(1);

    // Replace the word in the original array
    words[i] = capitalizedWord;
  }

  // Join the words back into a sentence
  const capitalizedSentence = words.join(' ');

  return capitalizedSentence;
}

