Here's a method to implement a Hashmap with the given parameters:

```java
public class HashMap {
    private static final int INITIAL_CAPACITY = 16;
    private static final double LOAD_FACTOR_THRESHOLD = 0.75;

    private Entry[] table;
    private int size;

    public HashMap() {
        table = new Entry[INITIAL_CAPACITY];
        size = 0;
    }

    public void put(String key, Integer value) {
        if (key == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Null keys are not allowed");
        }

        int hash = key.hashCode();
        int index = hash % table.length;

        Entry entry = table[index];

        while (entry != null) {
            if (entry.key.equals(key)) {
                entry.value = value;
                return;
            }
            entry = entry.next;
        }

        entry = new Entry(key, value);
        entry.next = table[index];
        table[index] = entry;
        size++;

        // Resize the table if load factor exceeds the threshold
        if (1.0 * size / table.length > LOAD_FACTOR_THRESHOLD) {
            resize();
        }
    }

    public Integer get(String key) {
        if (key == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Null keys are not allowed");
        }

        int hash = key.hashCode();
        int index = hash % table.length;

        Entry entry = table[index];

        while (entry != null) {
            if (entry.key.equals(key)) {
                return entry.value;
            }
            entry = entry.next;
        }

        return null;
    }

    private void resize() {
        Entry[] oldTable = table;
        int newCapacity = oldTable.length * 2;
        table = new Entry[newCapacity];
        size = 0;

        for (Entry entry : oldTable) {
            while (entry != null) {
                put(entry.key, entry.value);
                entry = entry.next;
            }
        }
    }

    private static class Entry {
        private String key;
        private Integer value;
        private Entry next;

        public Entry(String key, Integer value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
            next = null;
        }
    }
}
```

In this implementation, we use an array of Entry objects to store the key-value pairs. Each Entry object represents a linked list that handles collisions using chaining. The array is initially set to a fixed size, but when the load factor (the ratio of the number of elements to the array size) exceeds a threshold, the array is resized to twice its original capacity.

The put() method calculates the hash code of the key and uses it to find the corresponding index in the array. It then iterates through the linked list at that index, checking if the key already exists. If it does, it updates the value of the existing entry. If not, it creates a new entry and adds it to the beginning of the linked list. After adding or updating the entry, it checks the load factor and resizes the array if necessary.

The get() method also calculates the hash code of the key and finds the corresponding index. It then iterates through the linked list at that index, searching for the entry with the matching key. If found, it returns the corresponding value. If not, it returns null.

The resize() method creates a new array with double the capacity of the original array. It then iterates through each entry in the original array, rehashes it and adds it to the new array. This ensures that the elements are distributed more evenly across the new array.

Note that this implementation assumes that the hash codes of the keys are evenly distributed. In practice, it's a good idea to override the hashCode() method in the key class to improve the distribution. Additionally, this implementation does not handle collisions using open addressing or other methods, which might be more suitable for certain use cases.

