Here is a JavaScript program that extracts the domain name from a given URL, ensuring that the URL is valid and contains at least one subdomain:

```javascript
function extractDomain(url) {
  // Check if the URL is valid
  const validUrlRegex = /^(?:https?:\/\/)?(?:www\.)?[a-zA-Z0-9-]+(?:\.[a-zA-Z]+)+$/;
  if (!validUrlRegex.test(url)) {
    console.log("Invalid URL");
    return;
  }

  // Extract the domain name
  const domainRegex = /^(?:https?:\/\/)?(?:www\.)?([a-zA-Z0-9-]+(?:\.[a-zA-Z]+)+)$/;
  const match = domainRegex.exec(url);
  if (!match) {
    console.log("Invalid URL: No domain found");
    return;
  }

  const domain = match[1];
  const subdomainRegex = /^[a-zA-Z0-9-]+/;
  const subdomain = subdomainRegex.exec(domain);

  if (!subdomain) {
    console.log("Invalid URL: No subdomain found");
    return;
  }

  return subdomain[0];
}

// Example usage
console.log(extractDomain("https://www.example.com")); // Output: www
console.log(extractDomain("https://subdomain.example.com")); // Output: subdomain
console.log(extractDomain("https://example.com")); // Output: Invalid URL: No subdomain found
console.log(extractDomain("https://www.example")); // Output: Invalid URL
console.log(extractDomain("example.com")); // Output: Invalid URL: No subdomain found
```

The `extractDomain` function takes a URL as an argument and performs the following steps:

1. Checks if the URL is valid by using a regular expression `validUrlRegex`. The regular expression ensures that the URL starts with either `http://` or `https://`, optionally followed by `www.`, followed by one or more alphanumeric characters or hyphens, followed by a dot and one or more alphanumeric characters. If the URL is invalid, it logs an error message and returns.

2. If the URL is valid, it extracts the domain name using the regular expression `domainRegex`. The regular expression captures the domain name (without `http://`, `https://`, or `www.`) into a group. If no domain is found, it logs an error message and returns.

3. The function then checks if the domain name has a subdomain by using the regular expression `subdomainRegex`. The regular expression captures one or more alphanumeric characters or hyphens at the start of the domain name. If no subdomain is found, it logs an error message and returns.

4. Finally, it returns the extracted subdomain.

You can use this function to extract the subdomain from a given URL by calling `extractDomain(url)`, where `url` is the URL you want to extract the subdomain from. The function returns the subdomain or logs an error message if the URL is invalid or doesn't contain a subdomain.

