Here's a Node.js command that rotates an array by a given number of positions in place:

```javascript
function rotateArray(arr, positions) {
  if (arr.length === 0 || positions === 0) {
    return arr;
  }

  const n = arr.length;
  const gcd = (a, b) => (b === 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b));
  const numRotations = gcd(positions, n);

  for (let i = 0; i < numRotations; i++) {
    const temp = arr[i];
    let j = i;

    while (true) {
      const k = (j + positions) % n;

      if (k === i) {
        break;
      }

      arr[j] = arr[k];
      j = k;
    }

    arr[j] = temp;
  }

  return arr;
}

const arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const positions = 2;
console.log(rotateArray(arr, positions)); // Output: [4, 5, 1, 2, 3]
```

This command uses the Euclidean algorithm to calculate the greatest common divisor (gcd) of the number of positions and the length of the array. The gcd represents the number of rotations needed to bring the array back to its original order.

The command then performs the rotations in place using a temporary variable and a while loop. It calculates the new index for each element by adding the number of positions and taking the modulus of the length of the array.

Finally, the rotated array is returned.

