1. Class Declaration: It is the first component of a class, which starts with the keyword "class" followed by the class name. The class name should be in TitleCase, starting with a capital letter.

2. Class Variables (Fields): These are the variables declared within a class that represent the state or attributes of an object. Class variables can have different access modifiers like public, private, or protected. They are also known as member variables or instance variables.

3. Methods: Methods in a class define the behavior or actions that can be performed by objects of that class. They represent the operations that can be performed on the class's data. Methods can have different access modifiers and can either return a value or be void.

4. Constructors: A constructor is a special method that is used to initialize objects of a class. It has the same name as the class and does not have a return type. Constructors are called automatically when an object is created and can be used to set initial values to the class variables.

5. Access Modifiers: These are keywords used to specify the accessibility of the class members (variables and methods). Java provides four access modifiers: public, private, protected, and default. Public members can be accessed from anywhere, private members are only accessible within the class, protected members are accessible within the class and its subclasses, and default members are accessible within the same package.

6. Inheritance: Inheritance is a mechanism in Java that allows a class (subclass) to inherit properties and methods from another class (superclass). It facilitates code reuse and supports the concept of a hierarchical structure.

7. Interfaces: Interfaces define a contract for classes to implement. They contain a collection of method signatures that a class must define if it implements the interface. Interfaces provide a way to achieve multiple inheritance in Java.

8. Inner Classes: Inner classes are classes defined within another class. They can be static or non-static. Inner classes can access the variables and methods of the outer class and provide a way to logically group related classes.

