Inside the main function, we initialize the variables for counting the number of prime numbers (`primeCount`), the sum of prime numbers (`primeSum`), and the largest prime number and its position (`largestPrime`, `largestPrimeRow`, `largestPrimeCol`).

We then use nested loops to fill the 2D array `arr` with unique prime numbers. We start with the number 2 and check if it is prime using the `isPrime()` function. If it is prime, we assign it to the current element in the array and update the prime count, sum, and largest prime number and its position accordingly. Then, we increment the number and repeat the process until we fill the entire array.

Finally, we print the array, the sum of all prime numbers, the largest prime number, and its position.

Note: The time complexity of the `isPrime()` function is O(sqrt(N)), as it checks all numbers up to the square root of the given number. The time complexity of filling the 2D array is O(N^2), as we have nested loops iterating N times each.

