Here is an example of how you can create a custom data structure to store and retrieve strings without using the built-in List<T> class or any other built-in collection classes in C#:

```csharp
using System;

public class MyCustomList
{
    private string[] data;
    private int size;
    private const int InitialCapacity = 4;

    public MyCustomList()
    {
        data = new string[InitialCapacity];
        size = 0;
    }

    public void Add(string item)
    {
        if (size == data.Length)
        {
            ResizeArray();
        }

        data[size] = item;
        size++;
    }

    public string Get(int index)
    {
        if (index < 0 || index >= size)
        {
            throw new IndexOutOfRangeException();
        }

        return data[index];
    }

    private void ResizeArray()
    {
        int newCapacity = data.Length * 2;
        string[] newData = new string[newCapacity];
        Array.Copy(data, newData, data.Length);
        data = newData;
    }
}

public class Program
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        MyCustomList myList = new MyCustomList();
        myList.Add("Hello");
        myList.Add("World");
        myList.Add("Custom");
        myList.Add("List");

        for (int i = 0; i < myList.Size; i++)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(myList.Get(i));
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, we create a `MyCustomList` class that internally uses an array of strings to store the data. The `Add` method is used to add strings to the list, and it automatically resizes the array if it reaches its capacity. The `Get` method retrieves the string at a given index.

