```java
public static void optimizedBubbleSort(int[] arr) {
    int n = arr.length;
    boolean swapped;

    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        swapped = false;

        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < arr[j + 1]) {
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
                swapped = true;
            }
        }

        if (!swapped) {
            // If no swaps are made during a pass, the array is already sorted
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

Explanation:
1. The function takes an array of integers as input and initializes the length of the array to `n`.
2. A boolean variable `swapped` is used to keep track of whether any swaps were made during a pass through the array. It is initially set to `false`.
3. The first loop iterates `n - 1` times, where `n` is the length of the array. This is because after each pass, the largest element is already placed at the correct position, so we don't need to consider it in the next pass.
4. The second loop iterates `n - i - 1` times, where `i` is the current pass number. It compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order.
5. If any swaps are made during a pass, `swapped` is set to `true`.
6. After each pass, we check if `swapped` is still `false`. If it is `false`, it means that no swaps were made during the pass, indicating that the array is already sorted. In that case, we break out of the loop to optimize the algorithm.
7. The function does not return anything because the array is sorted in-place.

