Here is a C++ program that sorts a given array of integers in ascending order using the bubble sort algorithm:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>

// Function to perform bubble sort on the given array
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n, int& comparisons, int& swaps) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            comparisons++;
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                swaps++;
                std::swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    const int SIZE = 1000000;
    const int RANGE = 2000001; // (-1 million to 1 million, inclusive)

    int arr[SIZE];

    // Generate random numbers within the specified range
    srand(time(nullptr));
    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; i++) {
        arr[i] = (rand() % RANGE) - 1000000;
    }

    int comparisons = 0;
    int swaps = 0;

    // Call bubbleSort function
    bubbleSort(arr, SIZE, comparisons, swaps);

    // Print sorted array
    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; i++) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    // Print the number of comparisons and swaps
    std::cout << "Comparisons: " << comparisons << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Swaps: " << swaps << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This program uses the `bubbleSort` function to sort the given array `arr`. The function takes the array, its size, and two reference variables `comparisons` and `swaps` to keep track of the number of comparisons and swaps made during the sorting process.

In the `main` function, the program generates random numbers within the range of -1 million to 1 million, inclusive, and stores them in the array. Then, the `bubbleSort` function is called to sort the array. Finally, the sorted array is printed, followed by the number of comparisons and swaps made.

Note that with one million elements, the bubble sort algorithm might not be the most efficient choice, as it has a time complexity of O(n^2). If you need to sort larger arrays within a reasonable amount of time, you may consider using more efficient sorting algorithms such as quicksort or mergesort.

