Here's a JavaScript code that implements a binary search algorithm:

```javascript
function binarySearch(arr, target) {
  let left = 0;
  let right = arr.length - 1;
  let closest = null;

  while (left <= right) {
    let mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
    let midValue = arr[mid];

    if (midValue === target) {
      // Check for duplicate elements
      while (mid > 0 && arr[mid - 1] === target) {
        mid--;
      }
      return mid;
    }

    if (closest === null || Math.abs(midValue - target) < Math.abs(closest - target)) {
      closest = midValue;
    }

    if (midValue < target) {
      left = mid + 1;
    } else {
      right = mid - 1;
    }
  }

  return closest;
}

const arr = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11];
const target = 11;
console.log(binarySearch(arr, target));
```

This code performs a binary search on the `arr` array to find the first occurrence of the `target` value. It also handles non-integer values in the array by finding the closest value to the `target` value. The code returns the index of the first occurrence of the `target` value or the index of the closest value if the `target` value is not found in the array.

