Here is an algorithm in C++ that finds the process with the highest execution time without using any built-in sorting functions or algorithms:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// Define a Process structure
struct Process {
    std::string name;
    int id;
    int priority;
    int executionTime;
};

// Function to find the process with the highest execution time
Process findProcessWithHighestExecutionTime(Process* processes, int size) {
    // Initialize a variable to store the index of the process with the highest execution time
    int maxExecutionTimeIndex = 0;

    // Iterate through the array of processes
    for (int i = 1; i < size; i++) {
        // Check if the current process has a higher execution time than the process with the current maximum execution time
        if (processes[i].executionTime > processes[maxExecutionTimeIndex].executionTime) {
            maxExecutionTimeIndex = i; // Update the index of the process with the highest execution time
        }
    }

    // Return the process with the highest execution time
    return processes[maxExecutionTimeIndex];
}

int main() {
    // Example usage
    Process processes[] = {
        {"Process 1", 1, 2, 10},
        {"Process 2", 2, 1, 15},
        {"Process 3", 3, 3, 5},
        {"Process 4", 4, 2, 20}
    };

    int size = sizeof(processes) / sizeof(Process);

    Process highestExecutionTimeProcess = findProcessWithHighestExecutionTime(processes, size);

    std::cout << "Process with the highest execution time: " << highestExecutionTimeProcess.name << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This algorithm iterates through the array of processes and keeps track of the index of the process with the highest execution time. It compares each process's execution time with the execution time of the current maximum and updates the index accordingly. Finally, it returns the process with the highest execution time.

