```python
def binary_search(arr, target):
    # Check if list contains only one item
    if len(arr) == 1:
        return arr[0] == target

    if len(arr) == 0:
        return False

    mid = len(arr) // 2
    if arr[mid] == target:
        return True
    elif arr[mid] < target:
        return binary_search(arr[mid+1:], target)
    else:
        return binary_search(arr[:mid], target)
```

In the optimized code, we first check if the list contains only one item or is empty, in which case we return the comparison result or False, respectively.

Otherwise, we calculate the middle index of the list and compare the middle element with the target. If they are equal, we return True. If the middle element is less than the target, we recursively call the function with the right half of the list. If the middle element is greater than the target, we recursively call the function with the left half of the list.

By halving the list in each recursive call, the time complexity of the binary search becomes logarithmic.

