```javascript
class OrderedSet {
  constructor() {
    this.set = new Set();
    this.head = null;
    this.tail = null;
  }

  add(element) {
    if (this.set.has(element)) {
      return;
    }

    const node = { value: element, prev: null, next: null };

    if (this.head === null) {
      this.head = node;
      this.tail = node;
    } else {
      node.prev = this.tail;
      this.tail.next = node;
      this.tail = node;
    }

    this.set.add(element);
  }

  has(element) {
    return this.set.has(element);
  }

  toArray() {
    let current = this.head;
    const result = [];

    while (current !== null) {
      result.push(current.value);
      current = current.next;
    }

    return result;
  }
}
```

With this implementation, you can add elements to the `OrderedSet` using the `add` method, check if an element exists in the set using the `has` method, and convert the set to an array while preserving the order using the `toArray` method.

