Here's a solution that reverses the order of elements in an array without using any built-in functions or creating a new array, while maintaining a time complexity of O(n) and using only a few integer variables:

1. Initialize two integer variables, "start" and "end", to represent the indices of the first and last elements in the array, respectively. Set "start" to 0 and "end" to the length of the array minus 1.

2. While "start" is less than "end", do the following steps:

   a. Swap the elements at indices "start" and "end" in the array. To do this, you can use a temporary variable to hold the value of the element at "start", then assign the value of the element at "end" to the element at "start", and finally assign the value of the temporary variable to the element at "end".

   b. Increment "start" by 1 and decrement "end" by 1.

3. After the loop, the array will be reversed.

Here's the code implementation in Java:

```
public static void reverseArray(int[] array) {
    int start = 0;
    int end = array.length - 1;

    while (start < end) {
        // Swap elements at indices start and end
        int temp = array[start];
        array[start] = array[end];
        array[end] = temp;

        start++;
        end--;
    }
}
```

This solution iterates through the array once, swapping elements from the outer ends towards the middle until the entire array is reversed. Since it only uses a few integer variables, the space complexity is constant and does not depend on the size of the input array.

Here's an algorithm in Java to print all the prime numbers between a given range, with an optimized time complexity:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PrimeNumbersInRange {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int start = 1; // Starting number of the range
        int end = 100; // Ending number of the range

        List<Integer> primes = findPrimesInRange(start, end);
        System.out.println("Prime numbers between " + start + " and " + end + ":");
        for (int prime : primes) {
            System.out.println(prime);
        }
    }

    public static List<Integer> findPrimesInRange(int start, int end) {
        List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int number = start; number <= end; number++) {
            if (isPrime(number)) {
                primes.add(number);
            }
        }

        return primes;
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int number) {
        if (number <= 1) {
            return false;
        }

        // Checking for divisibility up to the square root of the number
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
            if (number % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

In this algorithm, we iterate through each number within the given range and check if it's prime using the `isPrime` method. The `isPrime` method checks for divisibility of the number up to its square root, which helps minimize the time complexity. The prime numbers found are stored in a `List` and then printed.

