        for j in range(n):
            if adjacency_matrix[i][j] == 1:
                outgoing_edges[i].append((j, weights_matrix[i][j]))
                incoming_edges[j].append((i, weights_matrix[i][j]))

    return outgoing_edges, incoming_edges
```

This function takes two matrices as input - `adjacency_matrix` representing the adjacency matrix and `weights_matrix` representing the weights of each edge. Both matrices are assumed to have the same dimensions.

The function first initializes two empty dictionaries - `outgoing_edges` and `incoming_edges` - to store the adjacency lists for outgoing and incoming edges respectively.

It then iterates over each element in the adjacency matrix and checks if there is an edge between nodes `i` and `j`. If the value in the adjacency matrix at position `(i, j)` is `1`, it means there is an edge.

For each edge found, the function appends a tuple `(j, weights_matrix[i][j])` to the `outgoing_edges[i]` list, indicating that there is an edge from node `i` to node `j` with weight `weights_matrix[i][j]`. Similarly, it appends a tuple `(i, weights_matrix[i][j])` to the `incoming_edges[j]` list, indicating that there is an edge from node `i` to node `j` with weight `weights_matrix[i][j]`.

Finally, the function returns the two dictionaries `outgoing_edges` and `incoming_edges`, which contain the adjacency lists for outgoing and incoming edges respectively.

