In the given code, we have a `Parent` class with a `display()` method and a `Child` class that extends the `Parent` class and overrides the `display()` method. In the `Main` class, we create an object of the `Child` class but assign it to a reference variable of the `Parent` class. When we call the `display()` method using the `obj` reference, it calls the `display()` method of the `Parent` class instead of the `Child` class, which results in incorrect output. To fix this, we need to declare the `display()` method in the `Child` class with the `@Override` annotation to ensure it is correctly overridden.

