Types of inheritance supported in Java:

1. Single inheritance: In single inheritance, a class extends only one other class. 
Example:
```java
class Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Parent Class");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Child Class");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child obj = new Child();
        obj.display(); // Output: Child Class
    }
}
```

2. Multilevel inheritance: In multilevel inheritance, a class extends another derived class.
Example:
```java
class Grandparent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Grandparent Class");
    }
}

class Parent extends Grandparent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Parent Class");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Child Class");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child obj = new Child();
        obj.display(); // Output: Child Class
    }
}
```

3. Hierarchical inheritance: In hierarchical inheritance, multiple classes inherit from a single base class.
Example:
```java
class Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Parent Class");
    }
}

class Child1 extends Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Child1 Class");
    }
}

class Child2 extends Parent {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Child2 Class");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child1 obj1 = new Child1();
        obj1.display(); // Output: Child1 Class

        Child2 obj2 = new Child2();
        obj2.display(); // Output: Child2 Class
    }
}
```

4. Multiple inheritance (not directly supported in Java): Multiple inheritance refers to a class inheriting from multiple classes, which is not directly supported in Java. However, Java supports multiple inheritance through interfaces. 
Example:
```java
interface Interface1 {
    public void display1();
}

interface Interface2 {
    public void display2();
}

class Child implements Interface1, Interface2 {
    public void display1() {
        System.out.println("Interface1's method");
    }

    public void display2() {
        System.out.println("Interface2's method");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child obj = new Child();
        obj.display1(); // Output: Interface1's method
        obj.display2(); // Output: Interface2's method
    }
}
```

