Here's a JavaScript function that generates a random number between 1 and 1000 and returns the sum of all the digits in the generated number with a time complexity of O(log n):

```javascript
function generateRandomNumber() {
  const randomNumber = Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000) + 1;
  return sumOfDigits(randomNumber);
}

function sumOfDigits(number) {
  let sum = 0;
  let remainingNumber = number;

  while (remainingNumber > 0) {
    sum += remainingNumber % 10;
    remainingNumber = Math.floor(remainingNumber / 10);
  }

  return sum;
}

console.log(generateRandomNumber());
```

The `generateRandomNumber` function generates a random number between 1 and 1000 using `Math.random()` and `Math.floor()`, and then calls the `sumOfDigits` function to calculate the sum of its digits.

The `sumOfDigits` function takes a number as an argument and initializes a sum variable to 0. It then uses a while loop to iterate through each digit of the number. In each iteration, it adds the last digit of the number to the sum using the modulo operator (`%`), and then updates the number by removing the last digit using integer division (`Math.floor(number / 10)`). This process continues until the number becomes 0, at which point the sum of all the digits has been calculated and is returned.

Note that the time complexity of the `sumOfDigits` function is O(log n), where n is the number passed as an argument. This is because the number of digits in a number is logarithmic with respect to the number itself.

