In this example, the form uses the `required` attribute to make sure all fields are filled out before submission. Additionally, each input field has an associated `<span>` element that will display error messages if necessary.

The form also includes a reference to a JavaScript file called `validation.js`, which will handle the client-side validation. Here's an example implementation of the JavaScript validation:

```javascript
function validateForm() {
  var name = document.getElementById("name").value;
  var email = document.getElementById("email").value;
  var password = document.getElementById("password").value;
  var confirmPassword = document.getElementById("confirmPassword").value;

  var nameError = document.getElementById("nameError");
  var emailError = document.getElementById("emailError");
  var passwordError = document.getElementById("passwordError");
  var confirmPasswordError = document.getElementById("confirmPasswordError");

  var nameRegex = /^[A-Za-z]{2,30}$/;
  var emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
  var passwordRegex = /^(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*\d)(?=.*[@$!%*?&])[A-Za-z\d@$!%*?&]{10,50}$/;

  nameError.innerHTML = "";
  emailError.innerHTML = "";
  passwordError.innerHTML = "";
  confirmPasswordError.innerHTML = "";

  if (!name.match(nameRegex)) {
    nameError.innerHTML = "Name must contain only alphabetical characters and have a length between 2 and 30 characters.";
    return false;
  }

  if (email.length > 50 || !email.match(emailRegex)) {
    emailError.innerHTML = "Email address must be valid and not exceed 50 characters.";
    return false;
  }

  if (password.length > 50 || !password.match(passwordRegex)) {
    passwordError.innerHTML = "Password must be between 10 and 50 characters, and contain at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, one numeric digit, and one special character.";
    return false;
  }

  if (password !== confirmPassword) {
    confirmPasswordError.innerHTML = "Confirm password must match the original password.";
    return false;
  }

  return true;
}
```

This JavaScript function `validateForm()` is called when the form is submitted. It retrieves the values of the input fields, performs the necessary validation using regular expressions, and displays appropriate error messages if any validation fails. If all fields pass validation, the function returns `true` and the form is submitted. Otherwise, it returns `false` and the form submission is prevented.

